{"parentUuid":"f7d65728-d89b-44d8-b4f1-112a1fbbfe55","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgHXnN4BVybBGP5uzw837","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VVLUYbjndF4g2YUNjhHvnZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\src\\JusPrin\\.claude\\worktrees\\calibration\\calibration-report.md","offset":1,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":434,"cache_read_input_tokens":277973,"output_tokens":122,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":434,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":122,"cache_read_input_tokens":277973,"cache_creation_input_tokens":434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":434},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VVLUYbjndF4g2YUNjhHvnZ":{"file_path":"C:\\src\\JusPrin\\.claude\\worktrees\\calibration\\calibration-report.md","offset":1,"limit":100}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgHXnMj6B4t3JDxhowN3E","requestRef":"0194214a-4bb1-4f34-be24-998aedf9f540","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"fffa3bdd-7c2f-4a2d-80d1-902095259203","timestamp":"2026-10-22T15:43:06.353Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin\\.claude\\worktrees\\calibration","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"claude/calibration","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
{"parentUuid":"fffa3bdd-7c2f-4a2d-80d1-902095259203","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"de187d87-78e0-4fae-b609-5bd6538472df","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VVLUYbjndF4g2YUNjhHvnZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Calibration in JusPrin: what was built and how it was checked\n2\t\n3\tWritten 2026-10-21 for someone who has not followed the work. The branch is\n4\t`claude/calibration` in the worktree `.claude/worktrees/calibration`. Nothing\n5\thas been pushed. This report, the brief and the handoff are untracked on\n6\tpurpose.\n7\t\n8\t## The short version\n9\t\n10\tA person can now ask JusPrin to calibrate a filament. They can do it in\n11\tthree ways: from the printer chip, from a filament slot, or from a printer\n12\tcard on Home. Or they can just describe a print problem in the chat. The\n13\tassistant asks which test fits and makes one of three OrcaSlicer tests as a\n14\tproject of its own: a temperature tower, the flow-ratio test, or a\n15\tpressure-advance tower. It slices the test and sends it to the printer, then\n16\treads the person's photo of the result. It saves the best value into a named\n17\tcopy of the filament (\"Bambu PLA Basic @BBL A1M · Fake A1 mini calibrated\"),\n18\tnever into OrcaSlicer's stock preset.\n19\t\n20\tEverything was built and tested on the Windows machine only. macOS and Linux\n21\twere not built or run.\n22\t\n23\t## What was built, commit by commit\n24\t\n25\t1. **53d0fc4f4a — the `calibration_generate` tool.** The tool takes the test\n26\t   and its range, then does three things:\n27\t   - saves and closes the open project the same way opening a project does;\n28\t   - calls OrcaSlicer's own generator for that test;\n29\t   - reports what it made: the project name, the test objects, the range\n30\t     actually applied, and a sentence the assistant can repeat.\n31\t\n32\t   It is offered both in the app and over MCP (your answer to question 2).\n33\t\n34\t   Supporting changes in the same commit:\n35\t   - The workspace now says whether the open plate is a calibration test and\n36\t     what its steps are. This is seam (a), the one you chose.\n37\t   - When a save would also save changes the person had not saved yet, the\n38\t     message now names each one with its saved value. The assistant can then\n39\t     put the saved value back.\n40\t   - One small addition to an OrcaSlicer-owned file: `Plater::set_project_name`,\n41\t     a public forwarder to the private setter. A simulated rebase onto\n42\t     upstream 1bc39fd358 merged it with no conflicts.\n43\t\n44\t2. **bab11c8eb1 — the calibration skill.** `AgentUI/src/skills/calibration/SKILL.md`\n45\t   tells the assistant several things:\n46\t   - when a test is worth making;\n47\t   - the two facts to confirm first, with a \"Make the test / Not now\" choice;\n48\t   - how to read each test's photo, and how to name and save the result.\n49\t\n50\t   When the person is already on their own preset, the assistant asks whether\n51\t   to write into it or save a copy (your answer to question 1). The\n52\t   print-issues skill gained one line pointing to this skill.\n53\t\n54\t3. **c15aecfc5e — the three Calibrate… rows.**\n55\t   - The rows are on the printer chip menu, the filament slot menu and the\n56\t     Home card's menu.\n57\t   - Each one shows OrcaSlicer's `MessageDialog` first. The dialog names the\n58\t     project that will be saved and closed, and says the test's settings are\n59\t     temporary.\n60\t   - Then the row opens the project chat with a message in the person's\n61\t     voice. The assistant asks which test.\n62\t   - From a Home card, the skill has the assistant set that printer up first.\n63\t\n64\t4. **196daa08de — the tool guide.** The calibration table in\n65\t   `mcp-tool-extension-guide.md` now describes one tool, not two.\n66\t\n67\t5. **be98979009 — a crash fix found by the end-to-end run.** Driving the tool\n68\t   over MCP crashed the app.\n69\t   - **Cause:** opening the test's project clears the list of tool calls,\n70\t     which can move the calibration call's own record in memory. The code\n71\t     then read that record after the move.\n72\t   - **Fix:** it now reads what it needs first. The same mistake in the\n73\t     tool-running loop is also fixed.\n74\t   - **Test:** a new bridge test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n75\t   - **Also in this commit:** the tool's result no longer repeats OrcaSlicer's\n76\t     \"outside the build volume\" notice. That notice is about the full-height\n77\t     tower before the generator cuts it down.\n78\t\n79\t6. **947fef989f — `--fake-printer` for the MCP test fixture.** The shell\n80\t   harness can now start its MCP fixture on a saved Bambu A1 mini that is\n81\t   connected to the in-process fake printer. The Testing README says how.\n82\t\n83\t7. **a63d497e94 — a Windows fix for the shell harness.** The harness now\n84\t   ignores the `\\r` in Windows line endings when it reads the prompt and\n85\t   skill files. The prompt recorder now passes on Windows. The same change\n86\t   should also clear the old `project_request_carries_the_page_instructions`\n87\t   failure in the default run, but I have not rerun the default harness to\n88\t   confirm.\n89\t\n90\t8. **6dfd52a6ed — the two calibration prompt cases.** It also brings the\n91\t   suite in line with the app, which now offers `skill_read`. See \"The\n92\t   prompt tests\" below.\n93\t\n94\t## What was checked, and how\n95\t\n96\t| Check | Where | Result |\n97\t|---|---|---|\n98\t| Adapter harness: each of the three tests generated from a project with unsaved edits; the project was saved first; the new project is named; the plate holds the test; the print's settings match the request; a reopened test still reports its steps; saving into a named copy | `workspace_adapter_integration_harness`, Windows | All 15 calibration checks pass. One unrelated check fails, explained below |\n99\t| Contract tests for the range rules | `workspace_contract_tests` | All pass (44 cases) |\n100\t| Tool, MCP and skill tests | `agent_bridge_tests` | 382 of 383 pass. The failure was there before this work, explained below |"}]},"uuid":"c4820d73-9a4c-4dc0-b5ef-2126ede3abb4","timestamp":"2026-10-22T15:43:06.413Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\JusPrin\\.claude\\worktrees\\calibration\\calibration-report.md","content":"# Calibration in JusPrin: what was built and how it was checked\n\nWritten 2026-10-21 for someone who has not followed the work. The branch is\n`claude/calibration` in the worktree `.claude/worktrees/calibration`. Nothing\nhas been pushed. This report, the brief and the handoff are untracked on\npurpose.\n\n## The short version\n\nA person can now ask JusPrin to calibrate a filament. They can do it in\nthree ways: from the printer chip, from a filament slot, or from a printer\ncard on Home. Or they can just describe a print problem in the chat. The\nassistant asks which test fits and makes one of three OrcaSlicer tests as a\nproject of its own: a temperature tower, the flow-ratio test, or a\npressure-advance tower. It slices the test and sends it to the printer, then\nreads the person's photo of the result. It saves the best value into a named\ncopy of the filament (\"Bambu PLA Basic @BBL A1M · Fake A1 mini calibrated\"),\nnever into OrcaSlicer's stock preset.\n\nEverything was built and tested on the Windows machine only. macOS and Linux\nwere not built or run.\n\n## What was built, commit by commit\n\n1. **53d0fc4f4a — the `calibration_generate` tool.** The tool takes the test\n   and its range, then does three things:\n   - saves and closes the open project the same way opening a project does;\n   - calls OrcaSlicer's own generator for that test;\n   - reports what it made: the project name, the test objects, the range\n     actually applied, and a sentence the assistant can repeat.\n\n   It is offered both in the app and over MCP (your answer to question 2).\n\n   Supporting changes in the same commit:\n   - The workspace now says whether the open plate is a calibration test and\n     what its steps are. This is seam (a), the one you chose.\n   - When a save would also save changes the person had not saved yet, the\n     message now names each one with its saved value. The assistant can then\n     put the saved value back.\n   - One small addition to an OrcaSlicer-owned file: `Plater::set_project_name`,\n     a public forwarder to the private setter. A simulated rebase onto\n     upstream 1bc39fd358 merged it with no conflicts.\n\n2. **bab11c8eb1 — the calibration skill.** `AgentUI/src/skills/calibration/SKILL.md`\n   tells the assistant several things:\n   - when a test is worth making;\n   - the two facts to confirm first, with a \"Make the test / Not now\" choice;\n   - how to read each test's photo, and how to name and save the result.\n\n   When the person is already on their own preset, the assistant asks whether\n   to write into it or save a copy (your answer to question 1). The\n   print-issues skill gained one line pointing to this skill.\n\n3. **c15aecfc5e — the three Calibrate… rows.**\n   - The rows are on the printer chip menu, the filament slot menu and the\n     Home card's menu.\n   - Each one shows OrcaSlicer's `MessageDialog` first. The dialog names the\n     project that will be saved and closed, and says the test's settings are\n     temporary.\n   - Then the row opens the project chat with a message in the person's\n     voice. The assistant asks which test.\n   - From a Home card, the skill has the assistant set that printer up first.\n\n4. **196daa08de — the tool guide.** The calibration table in\n   `mcp-tool-extension-guide.md` now describes one tool, not two.\n\n5. **be98979009 — a crash fix found by the end-to-end run.** Driving the tool\n   over MCP crashed the app.\n   - **Cause:** opening the test's project clears the list of tool calls,\n     which can move the calibration call's own record in memory. The code\n     then read that record after the move.\n   - **Fix:** it now reads what it needs first. The same mistake in the\n     tool-running loop is also fixed.\n   - **Test:** a new bridge test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n   - **Also in this commit:** the tool's result no longer repeats OrcaSlicer's\n     \"outside the build volume\" notice. That notice is about the full-height\n     tower before the generator cuts it down.\n\n6. **947fef989f — `--fake-printer` for the MCP test fixture.** The shell\n   harness can now start its MCP fixture on a saved Bambu A1 mini that is\n   connected to the in-process fake printer. The Testing README says how.\n\n7. **a63d497e94 — a Windows fix for the shell harness.** The harness now\n   ignores the `\\r` in Windows line endings when it reads the prompt and\n   skill files. The prompt recorder now passes on Windows. The same change\n   should also clear the old `project_request_carries_the_page_instructions`\n   failure in the default run, but I have not rerun the default harness to\n   confirm.\n\n8. **6dfd52a6ed — the two calibration prompt cases.** It also brings the\n   suite in line with the app, which now offers `skill_read`. See \"The\n   prompt tests\" below.\n\n## What was checked, and how\n\n| Check | Where | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Adapter harness: each of the three tests generated from a project with unsaved edits; the project was saved first; the new project is named; the plate holds the test; the print's settings match the request; a reopened test still reports its steps; saving into a named copy | `workspace_adapter_integration_harness`, Windows | All 15 calibration checks pass. One unrelated check fails, explained below |\n| Contract tests for the range rules | `workspace_contract_tests` | All pass (44 cases) |\n| Tool, MCP and skill tests | `agent_bridge_tests` | 382 of 383 pass. The failure was there before this work, explained below |","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":238}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fffa3bdd-7c2f-4a2d-80d1-902095259203","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin\\.claude\\worktrees\\calibration","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"claude/calibration","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
