{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:57:59.101Z","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","content":"\n\n<pasted_content id=\"53a8\">\nThe calibration handoff and the canvas boards changed on 2026-10-22, after you started. Re-read C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md in full before writing more code; it is the single source and it now describes a different entry flow from the one you built. What changed, and where to look:\n\n1. The entry flow is now one popup, then an empty calibration project. The popup names the filament and printer, never a test (\"Calibrate Gray PETG on Prusa MK4?\", one primary action \"Start\"). On Start the app, not the agent, saves and releases the open project through the project_open path, creates an empty calibration project named \"Calibration · <filament> · <printer>\", marks it as a calibration project in the fork's project document, opens its chat with a handover system line (\"From <project> · <filament> on <printer> · temporary test settings\", plus the symptom when there is one), and starts the first turn with the calibration_started trigger. The agent asks \"which test?\" with reply chips only when the handover does not decide it, then generates into that same project. Read section 2 decision 9 and section 5.3. The flow you built, a popup about closing the project followed by the agent asking which test and a second project, is what this replaces; the posted sentence \"I'm fine with test being saved and closed for the test\" goes away with it.\n\n2. calibration_generate continues the project instead of replacing it. A verification session ran OrcaSlicer's three generators into an already-open empty project: they build a test identical to OrcaSlicer's own dialog path, but each one replaces the project all the same (new project id, new workspace session, empty chat), because new_project() gives the model a new auxiliary directory and ProjectPersistence starts a fresh identity on it. So the tool needs a carry-over in fork-owned code and no change in OrcaSlicer: a one-shot in Agent/ProjectPersistence so the next project boundary keeps the current document; the shell's release hook skipping its save while that one-shot is armed; the generator called inside the adapter's tool-load scope; Plater::set_project_filename(<the project's name>) before the first autosave, or the saved version keeps the generator's name; and an exemption to the rule in AgentHost.cpp about line 715 that drops a tool result whose session changed, so the generate call's result lands in the chat that asked. The tool takes no project name any more. It has one rule for both paths: if the open project is not a calibration project (the symptom path, called from the person's own chat), release it and create one first; then generate. Read section 3 fact 9, section 5.1, and the report it cites, calibration-generate-verification.md, which is beside the handoff in Downloads.\n\n3. The skill gained a trigger and two rules. Frontmatter carries trigger: calibration_started beside its description. Point 2: ask which test as reply choices only when the handover names none. Point 4: generate before changing any setting, because an unsaved preset edit made in the calibration project carries into the test. Read section 5.2.\n\n4. Choices under the agent's words are reply chips, never buttons; buttons belong only to cards drawn from tool results (the proposal, the receipt). Home's project cards do not change. Read decisions 10 and 11.\n\n5. The no-agent case for the rows is undecided and must not be built. Leave the row enabled and route it nowhere special until the setup-flow design session lands. Read the end of section 5.3 and open question 4.\n\n6. The verification bullets in section 6 changed: one project on Home after generating, identity and chat kept, name kept, the symptom path leaving the person's chat with the last word, prompt tests for \"which test?\" with and without a handover.\n\nOn the canvas (https://claude.ai/artifact/VCEppdwfT7v32W4beUxL1v, page \"Calibration · Printer & Filament\", version 114): board 3 is the new popup; board 4 shows the calibration project's chat with the handover line, the three test chips, the person's chip reply and the generated tower in the same project; board 2's caption says the symptom path skips the question; the page note states the flow. Boards 5 to 8 did not change.\n\nThen tell me, before coding: which parts of what you already built survive as they are, which need rework, and anything in the revised handoff that contradicts what you found in the code.\n</pasted_content id=\"53a8\">\n"}
