{"parentUuid":"d3e66204-f467-4d16-9353-f1914ef16a4b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJVrSmpxUwqyQLC3Mme3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VgZ7jKEmdHqXuTv5b3Kbci","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":502795,"cache_read_input_tokens":48064,"output_tokens":334,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":60},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":502795,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":334,"cache_read_input_tokens":48064,"cache_creation_input_tokens":502795,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":502795},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"previous_message_not_found"}},"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VgZ7jKEmdHqXuTv5b3Kbci":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgJVrRtVnfkxkLfiJrwAb","requestRef":"f6823299-46f8-4faf-ac1a-fd85a2b8f13d","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"eab5fd60-6ae4-4c5b-b3ae-3c27e00b8d9d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:58:43.049Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
{"parentUuid":"eab5fd60-6ae4-4c5b-b3ae-3c27e00b8d9d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"46b3d61b-0340-48d5-8c60-f5a20be79cd7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VgZ7jKEmdHqXuTv5b3Kbci","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Handoff: printer and filament calibration in JusPrin\n2\t\n3\tWritten 2026-10-21, revised 2026-10-22 for the one-popup flow, for the agent who implements calibration. Nothing is\n4\tcoded yet; this document records the decisions Kenneth made, the facts verified in OrcaSlicer's code (by reading and,\n5\tfor the flow, by running), the design on the canvas, and the smallest build that satisfies both. Read\n6\t`agent-docs/jusprin/README.md` and the documents it lists first; `fork-stewardship.md` governs every line you change\n7\toutside `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/`. This file is untracked on purpose: never commit it.\n8\t\n9\t## 1. What a calibration is, in one paragraph\n10\t\n11\tA person has a symptom (stringing, under-extrusion, bulging corners) or just wants a filament dialled in. One popup\n12\ttells them their project will be saved and closed; then they are in an empty calibration project of its own, whose\n13\tagent has the context handed over, picks the test with them (asking only when the symptom did not decide it), and\n14\tgenerates OrcaSlicer's own test geometry into that project. The person prints it and sends a photo, the agent reads\n15\tthe print, and the result is saved as a new preset whose name the person is told to use from now on. The product\n16\tdefinition's section 2.11 lists the eight agent steps; this handoff is how they map onto code.\n17\t\n18\t## 2. Decisions already made (Kenneth, 2026-10-21 and 2026-10-22)\n19\t\n20\t1. A calibration is a project of its own, as long as the person is told what will happen before their project is\n21\t   closed. Not a plate in the current project (see fact 1 below for why that is impossible without rewriting Orca).\n22\t2. First batch: temperature tower, flow rate, pressure advance. Nothing else in this iteration.\n23\t3. The outcome of every test is a new preset: a named copy of the filament (or of the process or printer preset for\n24\t   the tests that write there). The app does not switch any project to it. The receipt names the preset and the\n25\t   agent tells the person to pick it from now on.\n26\t4. No remembered \"return to the previous project\". The test project is left through Home like any project.\n27\t5. No per-test UI. Adding a test must be a declaration plus skill text, not page code.\n28\t6. The physical-print ledger: \"doesn't matter, whatever is the easiest\". Leave it alone.\n29\t7. Entry points are hard-coded: a \"Calibrate…\" row in the project header's printer chip menu, in the filament slot\n30\t   menu, and in the Home printer card's kebab. A symptom typed in the project chat leads to the same flow.\n31\t8. The confirmation before a calibration is the Agent page's own Confirmation dialog, the one it draws for \"Delete\n32\t   chat?\": a web dialog, not Orca's `MessageDialog` and not a new native dialog (2026-10-21).\n33\t9. **One popup, then the calibration project** (2026-10-22, confirmed after the verification in fact 9). The popup\n34\t   names the filament and printer, not a test. After it, JusPrin creates and opens an empty, named calibration project\n35\t   and its chat, with the context handed over. The agent in that chat picks the test, asking \"which test?\" only when\n36\t   the handover does not decide it, and generates into that same project. The first implementation's flow (a popup\n37\t   about closing the project, then the agent asking which test, then a second project) is what this replaces.\n38\t10. Home's project cards do not change: no slice state, filament or calibration result on a card. The test is told\n39\t    apart by its project name (2026-10-22).\n40\t11. Choices under the agent's words are Chat/Reply Choice chips, never buttons. Buttons inside the thread belong only\n41\t    to cards drawn from tool results, the proposal and the receipt (2026-10-22).\n42\t\n43\t## 3. Facts verified in the code\n44\t\n45\tFacts 1 to 8 were verified by reading on 2026-10-21; fact 9 by running code on 2026-10-22.\n46\t\n47\t1. **Every OrcaSlicer generator replaces the open project and edits project-wide presets.** `Plater::calib_pa`,\n48\t   `calib_temp`, `calib_flowrate`, `calib_max_vol_speed`, `calib_retraction`, `calib_VFA`, the input-shaping pair and\n49\t   `Calib_Cornering` (`src/slic3r/GUI/Plater.cpp` from about line 12677) each call `new_project()` and then write to\n50\t   `preset_bundle->prints/filaments/printers.get_edited_preset().config`. Plates have no presets of their own, so a\n51\t   test on a plate of an existing project would change every other plate. Do not try.\n52\t2. **The test's parameters are runtime-only.** The generator stores them on the current plate's `Print` with\n53\t   `set_calib_params` (`Print.hpp:1089`); `GCode.cpp` reads them per layer (temperature at line 4697). Nothing writes\n54\t   them to the 3MF, and loading a project resets the mode to none at `Plater.cpp:5891`. A saved temperature tower,\n55\t   reopened, slices as a plain tower at one temperature. The pressure-advance pattern is the exception: its per-layer\n56\t   commands go into the plate's custom G-code, which the 3MF does save. The PA pattern also hard-codes the first\n57\t   plate's origin (`Plater.cpp:12814`), which is right because the generator's `new_project()` selects plate 0.\n58\t3. **OrcaSlicer already treats one outcome as a new preset.** The Bambu wizard's save page writes flow results as a\n59\t   filament preset named \"<filament> Flow Rate Calibrated\" (`CalibrationWizardSavePage.cpp:37`). Its pressure-advance\n60\t   results go to the machine, not a preset. The SoftFever dialogs (`calib_dlg.cpp`) save nothing: the person types\n61\t   the value into a preset by hand.\n62\t4. **Saving a copy already exists and selects the copy.** `settings_apply_patch` with `persistAs` saves through\n63\t   Orca's own `Tab::save_preset` and asserts the copy is now selected\n64\t   (`Workspace/OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp:3356-3369`); the result carries `saved_as`. A stock preset cannot be\n65\t   overwritten and the preview says so with `read_only_preset` and a suggested copy name (`check_persist`, line\n66\t   2941). Whether a process-scope copy works end to end is unverified; filament and printer are in use today.\n67\t5. **Which preset each result belongs to** (from `Preset.cpp`'s option lists): nozzle temperature, flow ratio,\n68\t   pressure advance, retraction length (as a filament override) and max volumetric speed are filament settings; VFA\n69\t   writes wall speeds in the process preset; cornering writes junction deviation (process) or jerk limits (printer);\n70\t   input shaping and Bambu's measured pressure advance live on the machine and produce no preset.\n71\t6. **The skill mechanism is ready.** `AgentUI/src/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` with a `description:` in its frontmatter is\n72\t   listed to the model and read on demand through the `skill_read` tool (`Agent/ToolRegistry.cpp:1209`,\n73\t   `AgentHost.cpp:2467`); a skill may also carry a `trigger:` the app fires on an event (`skillParser.ts`). Text limit\n74\t   16 KiB; one skill per directory; name equals directory name.\n75\t7. **The project-replacing path already exists.** The `project_open` tool saves the open project to its version\n76\t   history, drops unsaved preset edits, and calls `m_plater.new_project(true, true, name)` with confirmations skipped\n77\t   (`OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp:2450-2467`, `ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp:1005`). Creating the calibration project\n78\t   takes this route, not a second one.\n79\t8. **The setup card is already data-driven.** `setup_record` (`ToolRegistry.cpp:1071`) titles the pinned card from a\n80\t   purpose and headline. A calibration project needs no new pinned card: the agent records the test as the purpose\n81\t   and the range as the headline.\n82\t9. **Generating into a project JusPrin already opened, verified by running** (report:\n83\t   `.claude/worktrees/kind-mestorf-a37ba6/calibration-generate-verification.md`, untracked; its probe, harness diff\n84\t   and log are in the shared notes folder `calibration-generate-probe/`). With the real adapter and shell, each of\n85\t   the three generators called in a fresh, empty, named JusPrin project:\n86\t   - shows no dialog, and builds a test identical to OrcaSlicer's own dialog path: same objects on plate 0, same\n87\t     per-object settings, same `calib_params` on the plate's print, same preset edits, and for the PA pattern the\n88\t     same per-layer custom G-code after `reslice()`;\n89\t   - but replaces the project all the same: the JusPrin project id, the workspace session and the chat document\n90\t     change, because `new_project()` gives the model a new auxiliary directory and `ProjectPersistence` treats that\n91\t     as a project boundary (`ProjectPersistence.cpp:181-185`, `start_fresh_identity`). An empty project that already\n92\t     holds a chat is saved by the shell's release hook and appears on Home as a separate project;\n93\t   - renames the project; `Plater::set_project_filename(name)` restores JusPrin's name, and the name survives a save\n94\t     only if it is set before the first autosave (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:515-519` fixes the name at the first version);\n95\t   - keeps an unsaved preset edit made in the empty project: it carries into the test, because JusPrin's release hook\n96\t     skips OrcaSlicer's keep-or-discard question (`Plater.cpp:12077`).\n97\t   So the one-popup flow needs the carry-over in 5.1 and no change to OrcaSlicer-owned code. The report recommends\n98\t   against a seam that lets a generator skip its own `new_project()`: three functions in `Plater.cpp`, and it would\n99\t   skip resets that keep the test identical to OrcaSlicer's.\n100\t\n101\t## 4. The design\n102\t\n103\tJusPrin v2 canvas, page \"Calibration · Printer & Filament\": https://claude.ai/artifact/VCEppdwfT7v32W4beUxL1v\n104\t(nine boards, `calibration-01…08` plus `05b`). The builder that drew them is outside the repo at\n105\t`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin/figma-mirror/tools/composer/build_calibration_page.py`.\n106\t\n107\t- Board 1: the three \"Calibrate…\" rows, drawn as the app's menus are built today with one row added to each.\n108\t- Board 2: a symptom in the project chat. The agent explains, picks the test, then says in its own words that the\n109\t  current project will be saved and closed, that the test's settings are temporary, and that nothing is written\n110\t  until the person chooses. The reply choice is the confirmation; the calibration project then opens with the test\n111\t  already chosen, so board 4's question is skipped.\n112\t- Board 3: the one popup the menu rows show, in the Agent page's Confirmation dialog. It names the filament and\n113\t  printer, not a test, and carries the two facts: the project is saved and closed, the test's settings are temporary.\n114\t- Board 4: the calibration project. Its chat opens with a system line naming where it started from and for which\n115\t  filament and printer; the agent asks which test with reply chips; the person's chip reply; then the agent's word\n116\t  that the tower is on the plate, what to print and what to photograph. The pinned card is the Setup summary as\n117\t  `setup_record` fills it after generating.\n118\t- Board 5 and 5b: the photo comes back, the agent names the block and why, and proposes one write through the\n119\t  ordinary settings preview rows plus the preset it is saved as. 5b shows the card is one shape with slots (value,\n120\t  scope, base preset and printer, setting old → new, the word for what the person picks) and three instances.\n121\t- Board 6: the receipt with Undo. The agent tells the person the preset name to pick from now on; nothing switches.\n122\t- Board 7: the test reopened later warns that the steps are not in the file and offers to regenerate, as reply chips.\n123\t- Board 8: the test on Home as an ordinary project. Its card reads \"Saved\" like every card today (Home knows a\n124\t  closed project only by name, thumbnail and whether a print is running; `OrcaHomeBackend.cpp` ~line 202). The test\n125\t  is told apart by its project name.\n126\t\n127\tThe strings on the boards are illustrations; the agent writes its own. Keep the two facts of boards 2 and 3 verbatim\n128\tin meaning: the project closes, the test's edits are temporary.\n129\t\n130\t## 5. The build: one tool, one skill, three rows, one carry-over, one optional seam\n131\t\n132\tDo not build: a `calibration_list` tool (the tests are static knowledge; the workspace already reports printer,\n133\tfilament and nozzle), per-test cards, a return hook, preset switching, or Bambu's automatic path. Correct the tool\n134\tguide (`agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`, the `calibration` table around line 561): the\n135\t`calibration_generate` row says \"onto a new plate\", which fact 1 rules out, and `calibration_list` is dropped.\n136\t\n137\t### 5.1 The tool: `calibration_generate`, and the carry-over it needs\n138\t\n139\tOne registered tool (`Agent/ToolRegistry.cpp`, handler in `ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp`, adapter work in\n140\t`Workspace/OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp`). Input: `test` (`temperature`, `flow`, `pressure_advance` for now) and the\n141\ttest's parameters as Orca's dialogs take them (temperature and pressure advance: `start`, `end`, `step`; pressure\n142\tadvance also `method` line/pattern/tower and `extruderType`; flow: `pass` 1 or 2 and `linear`). No project name: the\n143\tcalibration project is already named when the tool runs. Output: the project's name, the plate and object ids, the\n144\tparameters as applied (Orca clamps and substitutes; return what it did) and the new `sessionId`, as `project_open`\n145\treturns one. Mark it in-app only until the MCP side is wanted.\n146\t\n147\tThe tool has one rule, whichever path reached it. First it makes sure the open project is a calibration project: if\n148\tit is not (the symptom path, where the agent calls from the person's own project), it releases the open project the\n149\tway `project_open` does (version saved, unsaved preset edits dropped) and creates the empty calibration project\n150\tthrough that same path, named \"Calibration · <filament> · <printer>\" and marked as a calibration project in the\n151\tfork's own project document (`Agent/ProjectPersistence`). Then it generates into it, in one call, with the\n152\tcarry-over fact 9 showed is needed, all in fork-owned files:\n153\t\n154\t1. `ProjectPersistence` gets a one-shot \"the next project boundary continues this project\". While it is armed,\n155\t   `adopt_current_project` keeps the current document (project id, chats, setup record) instead of calling\n156\t   `start_fresh_identity`; `ProjectAutosave::adopt_project` then returns early because the id is unchanged\n157\t   (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:459`), so version history continues in one store.\n158\t2. The shell's release hook (`ShellController.cpp:420`) does not save while the one-shot is armed: the same project\n159\t   continues, so no separate empty project reaches Home.\n160\t3. The tool arms the one-shot, calls the matching `Plater::calib_*` inside the adapter's existing tool-load scope\n161\t   (`ToolLoad`, as `open_project` uses it) so the generator's model load is not reported as a file the person\n162\t   opened, then calls `Plater::set_project_filename(<the project's name>)` before returning, ahead of the first\n163\t   autosave, and returns the new `sessionId`.\n164\t4. `AgentHost` drops a tool activity whose session is no longer current (`AgentHost.cpp:715`, \"the last word of an\n165\t   action that replaced the project belongs to the project it closed\"). A generate call that continued the project's\n166\t   identity is exempt: its result is recorded in the chat that asked, because that chat is still the project's. From\n167\t   the symptom path the person's own chat keeps the last word as `project_open` does today, and the calibration\n168\t   project's chat starts with the handover line of 5.3. State both in the tool's code where that rule lives.\n169\t\n170\tNothing in OrcaSlicer-owned code changes for this.\n171\t\n172\t### 5.2 The skill: `AgentUI/src/skills/calibration/SKILL.md`\n173\t\n174\tFrontmatter: `name: calibration`, `description:` one line that makes the model read it when a print symptom points\n175\tat the material or when the person asks to calibrate, and `trigger: calibration_started`, which the app fires for the\n176\tfirst turn of a calibration project's chat (5.3) so the model has the skill without choosing it. Body, in sections:\n177\t\n178\t1. When a test is justified, and which symptoms point to which test (stringing and hairs: temperature first,\n179\t   retraction second; under-extrusion, gaps, bulging corners or top surface: flow; corner bulges and gap at seams at\n180\t   speed: pressure advance). Say when drying the spool or checking the nozzle comes first.\n181\t2. In a calibration project whose handover names no test: ask which test, as reply choices, with one line each on\n182\t   what it shows and the order to take them in from scratch (temperature, flow, pressure advance). When the handover\n183\t   names the test or the symptom decides it, do not ask.\n184\t3. From the person's own project (the symptom path): say the two facts (project saved and closed; temporary\n185\t   settings) and get a reply before calling `calibration_generate`. In a calibration project the popup has already\n186\t   said them: do not repeat them.\n187\t4. Generate before changing any setting. An unsaved preset edit made in the calibration project before generating\n188\t   carries into the test (fact 9), so the skill forbids it; after generating, the test's own edits are the\n189\t   generator's business.\n190\t5. Per test: the default range per material, taken from OrcaSlicer's own dialog defaults (`calib_dlg.cpp` has the\n191\t   PLA, ABS/ASA, PETG, PCTG, TPU, PA-CF, PET-CF sets for temperature); what the person should photograph; how to\n192\t   read the print; which setting the result writes and in which preset scope; how to name the copy.\n193\t6. After generating: call `setup_record` with the test as the purpose and the range as the headline, then tell the\n194\t   person to slice and print and what to photograph.\n195\t7. Reading the photo: name the best block or tile and why, name the runner-up, and propose one write with\n196\t   `settings_preview_patch` then `settings_apply_patch` with `persistAs`. Naming rule: \"<base> · <printer>\n197\t   calibrated\"; a second test on the same base writes into that copy, never a second name. If the person is already\n198\t   on their own non-stock preset, writing in place with no new name is an option Kenneth has not ruled on: ask in\n199\t   the handover, do not decide it in the skill.\n200\t8. After saving: say the preset's name, that this test uses it now, that other projects keep their filament until\n201\t   the person picks it, and offer the next test without starting it, as a reply choice.\n202\t9. A reopened test: the steps are not in the file (fact 2); offer \"Regenerate tower\" and \"Just look at it\" as reply\n203\t   choices, and answer the first by calling `calibration_generate` again with the same range.\n204\t\n205\tKeep conversation rules in the skill and one-tool rules in the tool description (`rule-lives-next-to-what-it-governs`\n206\tin memory). Add one line to `skills/print-issues/SKILL.md` pointing at this skill for material symptoms.\n207\t\n208\t### 5.3 The entry rows and the one popup\n209\t\n210\t- Project header, printer chip: `Shell/PrinterMenu.cpp` beside \"Printer settings…\" (about line 176).\n211\t- Project header, filament slot sub-list: `Shell/FilamentMenu.cpp` beside \"Filament settings…\" (about line 461).\n212\t- Home card kebab: `HomeUI/src/components/PrinterMenu.tsx`, the action through `Home/HomeHost.cpp` and\n213\t  `Home/OrcaHomeBackend.cpp` like `open_printer_settings`.\n214\t\n215\tA row shows the one popup of board 3: the design system's Confirmation dialog as the Agent page already draws it for\n216\t\"Delete chat?\" (`Dialog` in `AgentUI/src/components/ChatNavigation.tsx`, `.chat-dialog` in `styles.css`), a web\n217\tdialog, not Orca's `MessageDialog` and not a new native dialog. It names the filament and printer and carries the two\n218\tfacts; its primary action is \"Start\". Cancel leaves everything as it was. Orca's `MessageDialog` remains the rule for\n219\tgenuinely native prompts, and `wxMessageBox` is never used (memory: `use-orca-messagedialog`). Every colour and size\n220\tin the native rows comes from `ShellTheme` and the tokens.\n221\t\n222\tOn Start, the app, not the agent: saves the open project to its version history and releases it (the `project_open`\n223\tpath, fact 7), creates the empty calibration project named \"Calibration · <filament> · <printer>\" and marks it as a\n224\tcalibration project in the fork's project document, opens its chat, posts the handover as a system line the model\n225\treads as the app's own words (\"Started from <project> · calibrating <filament> on <printer> · the test's settings\n226\tare temporary\", with the symptom when the row came from a print-issue context), and starts the first turn with the\n227\t`calibration_started` trigger so the skill is in force. The agent then asks which test, or generates at once when the\n228\thandover decides it, by calling `calibration_generate` (5.1), which continues this same project.\n229\t\n230\tFrom the symptom path (board 2) the agent calls `calibration_generate` from the person's own chat after the reply\n231\tchoice; the tool creates the calibration project itself, as 5.1 says, and the handover line is posted the same way.\n232\t\n233\tWith no agent set up, what a row does is not decided: Kenneth rejected leaving Home for the workspace to show setup\n234\t(\"most users don't have a workspace open\"), and the setup flow is being consolidated in a separate design session.\n235\tUntil that lands, do not specify or build the no-agent case; leave the row enabled and route it to whatever that\n236\tsession decides.\n237\t\n238\t### 5.4 Optional seam: keep the test valid after a reload\n239\t\n240\tFact 2 means a reopened test is a plain tower. Two ways out, pick after measuring: (a) a workspace fact\n241\t`calibration.generatedThisSession` the skill reads, plus board 7's regenerate offer; or (b) store the `Calib_Params`\n242\twith the fork's own project data (`Agent/ProjectPersistence`) and re-apply `set_calib_params` on the plate's print\n243\tafter load. (b) removes board 7's warning entirely and is small if the load observer in `Workspace/FileLoads` can\n244\trun it; the preset edits already come back with the project. The PA pattern's custom G-code needs nothing.\n245\t\n246\t## 6. Verification required before calling it done\n247\t\n248\t- Adapter test (Catch2, `tests/workspace`), reusing the probe in `calibration-generate-probe/`: from a person's\n249\t  project with unsaved preset edits, the popup path creates the named calibration project (a version of the person's\n250\t  project saved first; their preset edit not in the calibration project); each of the three generators called\n251\t  through `calibration_generate` keeps the project id, the chat and the name, leaves Home's project count unchanged,\n252\t  puts the test objects on plate 0 with `calib_params` as requested, and matches the result of OrcaSlicer's own path\n253\t  on the same inputs. Then reload the saved test and assert what fact 2 says, or what seam (b) fixes.\n254\t- Symptom path test: `calibration_generate` called from the person's own project releases it (version saved), creates\n255\t  the calibration project, generates, and the person's chat keeps the last word while the calibration project's chat\n256\t  holds the handover line and the tool's result.\n257\t- Settings test: a preview with `persistAs` on a stock filament reports `read_only_preset` with the copy name;\n258\t  apply with the copy name saves and selects it; a second apply with the same copy name updates it (no second copy).\n259\t- Page tests (Vitest): the skill parses, is listed with its description, fires on `calibration_started`, and stays\n260\t  under the size limit.\n261\t- Prompt tests, like `tests/printer_prompt` and `tests/project_prompt`: a calibration project whose handover names\n262\t  no test gets exactly one \"which test?\" turn with three reply choices and no generate call; a handover that names\n263\t  the test gets a generate call with no question; a symptom message in a person's project leads to the two facts and\n264\t  a reply choice before any generate call; a photo turn leads to exactly one preview and one apply with the naming\n265\t  rule. Record requests with the shell harness; no live-model batch without Kenneth approving it (memory:\n266\t  `prompt-eval-spend-budget`).\n267\t- One shell-harness run with `--manual-mcp` driving `calibration_generate` and the settings tools end to end on the\n268\t  fake Bambu printer (`src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md`).\n269\t- The three rows show the popup; Cancel leaves the project untouched; Start lands in the calibration project's chat\n270\t  with the handover line and no second project on Home.\n271\t- Choices under the agent's words (\"Temperature tower\", \"Regenerate tower\", \"Just look at it\") are Chat/Reply Choice\n272\t  chips, as on boards 2, 4, 6 and 7. Buttons inside the thread belong only to cards drawn from tool results.\n273\t\n274\t## 7. Open questions for Kenneth\n275\t\n276\t1. Write in place when the person is already on their own preset, or always a named copy?\n277\t2. Should the generate tool be offered over MCP in this iteration, or in-app only?\n278\t3. Seam (a) or (b) in 5.4, once you have measured (b).\n279\t4. The no-agent case for the rows, pending the setup-flow design session.\n280\t5. Whether the tool should also refuse to generate while the edited presets are dirty, or the skill rule in 5.2\n281\t   point 4 is enough. (Memory `rare-failures-get-a-generic-error` argues against per-cause handling.)\n282\t\n283\t## 8. Pointers\n284\t\n285\t- Product definition 2.11, 3.3 and 4.2: `agent-docs/jusprin/product-definition.md`.\n286\t- Tool and skill rules: `agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`, sections \"Decide whether this should be a\n287\t  tool\" and \"Skills versus tools\".\n288\t- Verification report and probe: `.claude/worktrees/kind-mestorf-a37ba6/calibration-generate-verification.md` and\n289\t  the shared notes folder `calibration-generate-probe/` (`calibration_probe.inc`, `real_adapter_harness.diff`,\n290\t  `calib-run.log`).\n291\t- Memory notes: `calibration-design-decisions`, `use-orca-messagedialog`, `rule-lives-next-to-what-it-governs`,\n292\t  `prompt-eval-spend-budget`, `temp-docs-stay-untracked-and-visible`, `canvas-boards-from-bundle-rules`.\n293\t"}]},"uuid":"7f74cecc-642f-45dc-9c92-45c3b6505696","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:58:43.232Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md","content":"# Handoff: printer and filament calibration in JusPrin\n\nWritten 2026-10-21, revised 2026-10-22 for the one-popup flow, for the agent who implements calibration. Nothing is\ncoded yet; this document records the decisions Kenneth made, the facts verified in OrcaSlicer's code (by reading and,\nfor the flow, by running), the design on the canvas, and the smallest build that satisfies both. Read\n`agent-docs/jusprin/README.md` and the documents it lists first; `fork-stewardship.md` governs every line you change\noutside `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/`. This file is untracked on purpose: never commit it.\n\n## 1. What a calibration is, in one paragraph\n\nA person has a symptom (stringing, under-extrusion, bulging corners) or just wants a filament dialled in. One popup\ntells them their project will be saved and closed; then they are in an empty calibration project of its own, whose\nagent has the context handed over, picks the test with them (asking only when the symptom did not decide it), and\ngenerates OrcaSlicer's own test geometry into that project. The person prints it and sends a photo, the agent reads\nthe print, and the result is saved as a new preset whose name the person is told to use from now on. The product\ndefinition's section 2.11 lists the eight agent steps; this handoff is how they map onto code.\n\n## 2. Decisions already made (Kenneth, 2026-10-21 and 2026-10-22)\n\n1. A calibration is a project of its own, as long as the person is told what will happen before their project is\n   closed. Not a plate in the current project (see fact 1 below for why that is impossible without rewriting Orca).\n2. First batch: temperature tower, flow rate, pressure advance. Nothing else in this iteration.\n3. The outcome of every test is a new preset: a named copy of the filament (or of the process or printer preset for\n   the tests that write there). The app does not switch any project to it. The receipt names the preset and the\n   agent tells the person to pick it from now on.\n4. No remembered \"return to the previous project\". The test project is left through Home like any project.\n5. No per-test UI. Adding a test must be a declaration plus skill text, not page code.\n6. The physical-print ledger: \"doesn't matter, whatever is the easiest\". Leave it alone.\n7. Entry points are hard-coded: a \"Calibrate…\" row in the project header's printer chip menu, in the filament slot\n   menu, and in the Home printer card's kebab. A symptom typed in the project chat leads to the same flow.\n8. The confirmation before a calibration is the Agent page's own Confirmation dialog, the one it draws for \"Delete\n   chat?\": a web dialog, not Orca's `MessageDialog` and not a new native dialog (2026-10-21).\n9. **One popup, then the calibration project** (2026-10-22, confirmed after the verification in fact 9). The popup\n   names the filament and printer, not a test. After it, JusPrin creates and opens an empty, named calibration project\n   and its chat, with the context handed over. The agent in that chat picks the test, asking \"which test?\" only when\n   the handover does not decide it, and generates into that same project. The first implementation's flow (a popup\n   about closing the project, then the agent asking which test, then a second project) is what this replaces.\n10. Home's project cards do not change: no slice state, filament or calibration result on a card. The test is told\n    apart by its project name (2026-10-22).\n11. Choices under the agent's words are Chat/Reply Choice chips, never buttons. Buttons inside the thread belong only\n    to cards drawn from tool results, the proposal and the receipt (2026-10-22).\n\n## 3. Facts verified in the code\n\nFacts 1 to 8 were verified by reading on 2026-10-21; fact 9 by running code on 2026-10-22.\n\n1. **Every OrcaSlicer generator replaces the open project and edits project-wide presets.** `Plater::calib_pa`,\n   `calib_temp`, `calib_flowrate`, `calib_max_vol_speed`, `calib_retraction`, `calib_VFA`, the input-shaping pair and\n   `Calib_Cornering` (`src/slic3r/GUI/Plater.cpp` from about line 12677) each call `new_project()` and then write to\n   `preset_bundle->prints/filaments/printers.get_edited_preset().config`. Plates have no presets of their own, so a\n   test on a plate of an existing project would change every other plate. Do not try.\n2. **The test's parameters are runtime-only.** The generator stores them on the current plate's `Print` with\n   `set_calib_params` (`Print.hpp:1089`); `GCode.cpp` reads them per layer (temperature at line 4697). Nothing writes\n   them to the 3MF, and loading a project resets the mode to none at `Plater.cpp:5891`. A saved temperature tower,\n   reopened, slices as a plain tower at one temperature. The pressure-advance pattern is the exception: its per-layer\n   commands go into the plate's custom G-code, which the 3MF does save. The PA pattern also hard-codes the first\n   plate's origin (`Plater.cpp:12814`), which is right because the generator's `new_project()` selects plate 0.\n3. **OrcaSlicer already treats one outcome as a new preset.** The Bambu wizard's save page writes flow results as a\n   filament preset named \"<filament> Flow Rate Calibrated\" (`CalibrationWizardSavePage.cpp:37`). Its pressure-advance\n   results go to the machine, not a preset. The SoftFever dialogs (`calib_dlg.cpp`) save nothing: the person types\n   the value into a preset by hand.\n4. **Saving a copy already exists and selects the copy.** `settings_apply_patch` with `persistAs` saves through\n   Orca's own `Tab::save_preset` and asserts the copy is now selected\n   (`Workspace/OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp:3356-3369`); the result carries `saved_as`. A stock preset cannot be\n   overwritten and the preview says so with `read_only_preset` and a suggested copy name (`check_persist`, line\n   2941). Whether a process-scope copy works end to end is unverified; filament and printer are in use today.\n5. **Which preset each result belongs to** (from `Preset.cpp`'s option lists): nozzle temperature, flow ratio,\n   pressure advance, retraction length (as a filament override) and max volumetric speed are filament settings; VFA\n   writes wall speeds in the process preset; cornering writes junction deviation (process) or jerk limits (printer);\n   input shaping and Bambu's measured pressure advance live on the machine and produce no preset.\n6. **The skill mechanism is ready.** `AgentUI/src/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` with a `description:` in its frontmatter is\n   listed to the model and read on demand through the `skill_read` tool (`Agent/ToolRegistry.cpp:1209`,\n   `AgentHost.cpp:2467`); a skill may also carry a `trigger:` the app fires on an event (`skillParser.ts`). Text limit\n   16 KiB; one skill per directory; name equals directory name.\n7. **The project-replacing path already exists.** The `project_open` tool saves the open project to its version\n   history, drops unsaved preset edits, and calls `m_plater.new_project(true, true, name)` with confirmations skipped\n   (`OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp:2450-2467`, `ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp:1005`). Creating the calibration project\n   takes this route, not a second one.\n8. **The setup card is already data-driven.** `setup_record` (`ToolRegistry.cpp:1071`) titles the pinned card from a\n   purpose and headline. A calibration project needs no new pinned card: the agent records the test as the purpose\n   and the range as the headline.\n9. **Generating into a project JusPrin already opened, verified by running** (report:\n   `.claude/worktrees/kind-mestorf-a37ba6/calibration-generate-verification.md`, untracked; its probe, harness diff\n   and log are in the shared notes folder `calibration-generate-probe/`). With the real adapter and shell, each of\n   the three generators called in a fresh, empty, named JusPrin project:\n   - shows no dialog, and builds a test identical to OrcaSlicer's own dialog path: same objects on plate 0, same\n     per-object settings, same `calib_params` on the plate's print, same preset edits, and for the PA pattern the\n     same per-layer custom G-code after `reslice()`;\n   - but replaces the project all the same: the JusPrin project id, the workspace session and the chat document\n     change, because `new_project()` gives the model a new auxiliary directory and `ProjectPersistence` treats that\n     as a project boundary (`ProjectPersistence.cpp:181-185`, `start_fresh_identity`). An empty project that already\n     holds a chat is saved by the shell's release hook and appears on Home as a separate project;\n   - renames the project; `Plater::set_project_filename(name)` restores JusPrin's name, and the name survives a save\n     only if it is set before the first autosave (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:515-519` fixes the name at the first version);\n   - keeps an unsaved preset edit made in the empty project: it carries into the test, because JusPrin's release hook\n     skips OrcaSlicer's keep-or-discard question (`Plater.cpp:12077`).\n   So the one-popup flow needs the carry-over in 5.1 and no change to OrcaSlicer-owned code. The report recommends\n   against a seam that lets a generator skip its own `new_project()`: three functions in `Plater.cpp`, and it would\n   skip resets that keep the test identical to OrcaSlicer's.\n\n## 4. The design\n\nJusPrin v2 canvas, page \"Calibration · Printer & Filament\": https://claude.ai/artifact/VCEppdwfT7v32W4beUxL1v\n(nine boards, `calibration-01…08` plus `05b`). The builder that drew them is outside the repo at\n`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin/figma-mirror/tools/composer/build_calibration_page.py`.\n\n- Board 1: the three \"Calibrate…\" rows, drawn as the app's menus are built today with one row added to each.\n- Board 2: a symptom in the project chat. The agent explains, picks the test, then says in its own words that the\n  current project will be saved and closed, that the test's settings are temporary, and that nothing is written\n  until the person chooses. The reply choice is the confirmation; the calibration project then opens with the test\n  already chosen, so board 4's question is skipped.\n- Board 3: the one popup the menu rows show, in the Agent page's Confirmation dialog. It names the filament and\n  printer, not a test, and carries the two facts: the project is saved and closed, the test's settings are temporary.\n- Board 4: the calibration project. Its chat opens with a system line naming where it started from and for which\n  filament and printer; the agent asks which test with reply chips; the person's chip reply; then the agent's word\n  that the tower is on the plate, what to print and what to photograph. The pinned card is the Setup summary as\n  `setup_record` fills it after generating.\n- Board 5 and 5b: the photo comes back, the agent names the block and why, and proposes one write through the\n  ordinary settings preview rows plus the preset it is saved as. 5b shows the card is one shape with slots (value,\n  scope, base preset and printer, setting old → new, the word for what the person picks) and three instances.\n- Board 6: the receipt with Undo. The agent tells the person the preset name to pick from now on; nothing switches.\n- Board 7: the test reopened later warns that the steps are not in the file and offers to regenerate, as reply chips.\n- Board 8: the test on Home as an ordinary project. Its card reads \"Saved\" like every card today (Home knows a\n  closed project only by name, thumbnail and whether a print is running; `OrcaHomeBackend.cpp` ~line 202). The test\n  is told apart by its project name.\n\nThe strings on the boards are illustrations; the agent writes its own. Keep the two facts of boards 2 and 3 verbatim\nin meaning: the project closes, the test's edits are temporary.\n\n## 5. The build: one tool, one skill, three rows, one carry-over, one optional seam\n\nDo not build: a `calibration_list` tool (the tests are static knowledge; the workspace already reports printer,\nfilament and nozzle), per-test cards, a return hook, preset switching, or Bambu's automatic path. Correct the tool\nguide (`agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`, the `calibration` table around line 561): the\n`calibration_generate` row says \"onto a new plate\", which fact 1 rules out, and `calibration_list` is dropped.\n\n### 5.1 The tool: `calibration_generate`, and the carry-over it needs\n\nOne registered tool (`Agent/ToolRegistry.cpp`, handler in `ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp`, adapter work in\n`Workspace/OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp`). Input: `test` (`temperature`, `flow`, `pressure_advance` for now) and the\ntest's parameters as Orca's dialogs take them (temperature and pressure advance: `start`, `end`, `step`; pressure\nadvance also `method` line/pattern/tower and `extruderType`; flow: `pass` 1 or 2 and `linear`). No project name: the\ncalibration project is already named when the tool runs. Output: the project's name, the plate and object ids, the\nparameters as applied (Orca clamps and substitutes; return what it did) and the new `sessionId`, as `project_open`\nreturns one. Mark it in-app only until the MCP side is wanted.\n\nThe tool has one rule, whichever path reached it. First it makes sure the open project is a calibration project: if\nit is not (the symptom path, where the agent calls from the person's own project), it releases the open project the\nway `project_open` does (version saved, unsaved preset edits dropped) and creates the empty calibration project\nthrough that same path, named \"Calibration · <filament> · <printer>\" and marked as a calibration project in the\nfork's own project document (`Agent/ProjectPersistence`). Then it generates into it, in one call, with the\ncarry-over fact 9 showed is needed, all in fork-owned files:\n\n1. `ProjectPersistence` gets a one-shot \"the next project boundary continues this project\". While it is armed,\n   `adopt_current_project` keeps the current document (project id, chats, setup record) instead of calling\n   `start_fresh_identity`; `ProjectAutosave::adopt_project` then returns early because the id is unchanged\n   (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:459`), so version history continues in one store.\n2. The shell's release hook (`ShellController.cpp:420`) does not save while the one-shot is armed: the same project\n   continues, so no separate empty project reaches Home.\n3. The tool arms the one-shot, calls the matching `Plater::calib_*` inside the adapter's existing tool-load scope\n   (`ToolLoad`, as `open_project` uses it) so the generator's model load is not reported as a file the person\n   opened, then calls `Plater::set_project_filename(<the project's name>)` before returning, ahead of the first\n   autosave, and returns the new `sessionId`.\n4. `AgentHost` drops a tool activity whose session is no longer current (`AgentHost.cpp:715`, \"the last word of an\n   action that replaced the project belongs to the project it closed\"). A generate call that continued the project's\n   identity is exempt: its result is recorded in the chat that asked, because that chat is still the project's. From\n   the symptom path the person's own chat keeps the last word as `project_open` does today, and the calibration\n   project's chat starts with the handover line of 5.3. State both in the tool's code where that rule lives.\n\nNothing in OrcaSlicer-owned code changes for this.\n\n### 5.2 The skill: `AgentUI/src/skills/calibration/SKILL.md`\n\nFrontmatter: `name: calibration`, `description:` one line that makes the model read it when a print symptom points\nat the material or when the person asks to calibrate, and `trigger: calibration_started`, which the app fires for the\nfirst turn of a calibration project's chat (5.3) so the model has the skill without choosing it. Body, in sections:\n\n1. When a test is justified, and which symptoms point to which test (stringing and hairs: temperature first,\n   retraction second; under-extrusion, gaps, bulging corners or top surface: flow; corner bulges and gap at seams at\n   speed: pressure advance). Say when drying the spool or checking the nozzle comes first.\n2. In a calibration project whose handover names no test: ask which test, as reply choices, with one line each on\n   what it shows and the order to take them in from scratch (temperature, flow, pressure advance). When the handover\n   names the test or the symptom decides it, do not ask.\n3. From the person's own project (the symptom path): say the two facts (project saved and closed; temporary\n   settings) and get a reply before calling `calibration_generate`. In a calibration project the popup has already\n   said them: do not repeat them.\n4. Generate before changing any setting. An unsaved preset edit made in the calibration project before generating\n   carries into the test (fact 9), so the skill forbids it; after generating, the test's own edits are the\n   generator's business.\n5. Per test: the default range per material, taken from OrcaSlicer's own dialog defaults (`calib_dlg.cpp` has the\n   PLA, ABS/ASA, PETG, PCTG, TPU, PA-CF, PET-CF sets for temperature); what the person should photograph; how to\n   read the print; which setting the result writes and in which preset scope; how to name the copy.\n6. After generating: call `setup_record` with the test as the purpose and the range as the headline, then tell the\n   person to slice and print and what to photograph.\n7. Reading the photo: name the best block or tile and why, name the runner-up, and propose one write with\n   `settings_preview_patch` then `settings_apply_patch` with `persistAs`. Naming rule: \"<base> · <printer>\n   calibrated\"; a second test on the same base writes into that copy, never a second name. If the person is already\n   on their own non-stock preset, writing in place with no new name is an option Kenneth has not ruled on: ask in\n   the handover, do not decide it in the skill.\n8. After saving: say the preset's name, that this test uses it now, that other projects keep their filament until\n   the person picks it, and offer the next test without starting it, as a reply choice.\n9. A reopened test: the steps are not in the file (fact 2); offer \"Regenerate tower\" and \"Just look at it\" as reply\n   choices, and answer the first by calling `calibration_generate` again with the same range.\n\nKeep conversation rules in the skill and one-tool rules in the tool description (`rule-lives-next-to-what-it-governs`\nin memory). Add one line to `skills/print-issues/SKILL.md` pointing at this skill for material symptoms.\n\n### 5.3 The entry rows and the one popup\n\n- Project header, printer chip: `Shell/PrinterMenu.cpp` beside \"Printer settings…\" (about line 176).\n- Project header, filament slot sub-list: `Shell/FilamentMenu.cpp` beside \"Filament settings…\" (about line 461).\n- Home card kebab: `HomeUI/src/components/PrinterMenu.tsx`, the action through `Home/HomeHost.cpp` and\n  `Home/OrcaHomeBackend.cpp` like `open_printer_settings`.\n\nA row shows the one popup of board 3: the design system's Confirmation dialog as the Agent page already draws it for\n\"Delete chat?\" (`Dialog` in `AgentUI/src/components/ChatNavigation.tsx`, `.chat-dialog` in `styles.css`), a web\ndialog, not Orca's `MessageDialog` and not a new native dialog. It names the filament and printer and carries the two\nfacts; its primary action is \"Start\". Cancel leaves everything as it was. Orca's `MessageDialog` remains the rule for\ngenuinely native prompts, and `wxMessageBox` is never used (memory: `use-orca-messagedialog`). Every colour and size\nin the native rows comes from `ShellTheme` and the tokens.\n\nOn Start, the app, not the agent: saves the open project to its version history and releases it (the `project_open`\npath, fact 7), creates the empty calibration project named \"Calibration · <filament> · <printer>\" and marks it as a\ncalibration project in the fork's project document, opens its chat, posts the handover as a system line the model\nreads as the app's own words (\"Started from <project> · calibrating <filament> on <printer> · the test's settings\nare temporary\", with the symptom when the row came from a print-issue context), and starts the first turn with the\n`calibration_started` trigger so the skill is in force. The agent then asks which test, or generates at once when the\nhandover decides it, by calling `calibration_generate` (5.1), which continues this same project.\n\nFrom the symptom path (board 2) the agent calls `calibration_generate` from the person's own chat after the reply\nchoice; the tool creates the calibration project itself, as 5.1 says, and the handover line is posted the same way.\n\nWith no agent set up, what a row does is not decided: Kenneth rejected leaving Home for the workspace to show setup\n(\"most users don't have a workspace open\"), and the setup flow is being consolidated in a separate design session.\nUntil that lands, do not specify or build the no-agent case; leave the row enabled and route it to whatever that\nsession decides.\n\n### 5.4 Optional seam: keep the test valid after a reload\n\nFact 2 means a reopened test is a plain tower. Two ways out, pick after measuring: (a) a workspace fact\n`calibration.generatedThisSession` the skill reads, plus board 7's regenerate offer; or (b) store the `Calib_Params`\nwith the fork's own project data (`Agent/ProjectPersistence`) and re-apply `set_calib_params` on the plate's print\nafter load. (b) removes board 7's warning entirely and is small if the load observer in `Workspace/FileLoads` can\nrun it; the preset edits already come back with the project. The PA pattern's custom G-code needs nothing.\n\n## 6. Verification required before calling it done\n\n- Adapter test (Catch2, `tests/workspace`), reusing the probe in `calibration-generate-probe/`: from a person's\n  project with unsaved preset edits, the popup path creates the named calibration project (a version of the person's\n  project saved first; their preset edit not in the calibration project); each of the three generators called\n  through `calibration_generate` keeps the project id, the chat and the name, leaves Home's project count unchanged,\n  puts the test objects on plate 0 with `calib_params` as requested, and matches the result of OrcaSlicer's own path\n  on the same inputs. Then reload the saved test and assert what fact 2 says, or what seam (b) fixes.\n- Symptom path test: `calibration_generate` called from the person's own project releases it (version saved), creates\n  the calibration project, generates, and the person's chat keeps the last word while the calibration project's chat\n  holds the handover line and the tool's result.\n- Settings test: a preview with `persistAs` on a stock filament reports `read_only_preset` with the copy name;\n  apply with the copy name saves and selects it; a second apply with the same copy name updates it (no second copy).\n- Page tests (Vitest): the skill parses, is listed with its description, fires on `calibration_started`, and stays\n  under the size limit.\n- Prompt tests, like `tests/printer_prompt` and `tests/project_prompt`: a calibration project whose handover names\n  no test gets exactly one \"which test?\" turn with three reply choices and no generate call; a handover that names\n  the test gets a generate call with no question; a symptom message in a person's project leads to the two facts and\n  a reply choice before any generate call; a photo turn leads to exactly one preview and one apply with the naming\n  rule. Record requests with the shell harness; no live-model batch without Kenneth approving it (memory:\n  `prompt-eval-spend-budget`).\n- One shell-harness run with `--manual-mcp` driving `calibration_generate` and the settings tools end to end on the\n  fake Bambu printer (`src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md`).\n- The three rows show the popup; Cancel leaves the project untouched; Start lands in the calibration project's chat\n  with the handover line and no second project on Home.\n- Choices under the agent's words (\"Temperature tower\", \"Regenerate tower\", \"Just look at it\") are Chat/Reply Choice\n  chips, as on boards 2, 4, 6 and 7. Buttons inside the thread belong only to cards drawn from tool results.\n\n## 7. Open questions for Kenneth\n\n1. Write in place when the person is already on their own preset, or always a named copy?\n2. Should the generate tool be offered over MCP in this iteration, or in-app only?\n3. Seam (a) or (b) in 5.4, once you have measured (b).\n4. The no-agent case for the rows, pending the setup-flow design session.\n5. Whether the tool should also refuse to generate while the edited presets are dirty, or the skill rule in 5.2\n   point 4 is enough. (Memory `rare-failures-get-a-generic-error` argues against per-cause handling.)\n\n## 8. Pointers\n\n- Product definition 2.11, 3.3 and 4.2: `agent-docs/jusprin/product-definition.md`.\n- Tool and skill rules: `agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`, sections \"Decide whether this should be a\n  tool\" and \"Skills versus tools\".\n- Verification report and probe: `.claude/worktrees/kind-mestorf-a37ba6/calibration-generate-verification.md` and\n  the shared notes folder `calibration-generate-probe/` (`calibration_probe.inc`, `real_adapter_harness.diff`,\n  `calib-run.log`).\n- Memory notes: `calibration-design-decisions`, `use-orca-messagedialog`, `rule-lives-next-to-what-it-governs`,\n  `prompt-eval-spend-budget`, `temp-docs-stay-untracked-and-visible`, `canvas-boards-from-bundle-rules`.\n","numLines":293,"startLine":1,"totalLines":293}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"eab5fd60-6ae4-4c5b-b3ae-3c27e00b8d9d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
