{"parentUuid":"70c9cb77-8373-45f8-8625-7ff4c81670d6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJVvg9MzwcC3q7oQRT1t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MfGndiWhKUQN2QmEHnAr7F","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-generate-verification.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1801,"cache_read_input_tokens":560869,"output_tokens":252,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":109},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1801,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":252,"cache_read_input_tokens":560869,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1801,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1801},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01MfGndiWhKUQN2QmEHnAr7F":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-generate-verification.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgJVvfpGA5fdrHZth3GUz","requestRef":"849d0eea-ce74-4933-ba17-ca2ced7ddc83","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"5886aee2-f4e3-450a-ade7-c92337fba237","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:59:23.090Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
{"parentUuid":"5886aee2-f4e3-450a-ade7-c92337fba237","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"46b3d61b-0340-48d5-8c60-f5a20be79cd7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MfGndiWhKUQN2QmEHnAr7F","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Can OrcaSlicer's calibration generators run into a project JusPrin already opened?\n2\t\n3\tVerified by running code on 2026-10-22. Untracked working document; do not commit.\n4\t\n5\t## Short answer\n6\t\n7\t**No, not as the flow is worded today; yes with one JusPrin-owned addition, and no OrcaSlicer change is needed.**\n8\t\n9\tOrcaSlicer's temperature, flow and pressure-advance generators never draw into the project that is open. Each one\n10\tstarts its own new project. They do this silently: no dialog appears, and the test they build is identical to what\n11\tOrcaSlicer's own dialogs build. But the project that holds the test is a different JusPrin project from the empty one\n12\tJusPrin opened. It has a new project id, a new workspace session and an empty chat. If the empty project already had a\n13\tchat (the agent asked \"which test?\"), that empty project is also saved and shows up on Home as a separate project.\n14\t\n15\tSo \"one popup → empty calibration project → the agent asks which test → generate into this project\" works only if\n16\tJusPrin carries its own project identity (id, chat, name) across the generator's project replacement. That is a change\n17\tin fork-owned code (`ProjectPersistence`, the release hook in `ShellController`, the generate tool), described at the end.\n18\t\n19\t## The hypothesis tested\n20\t\n21\tThe flow Kenneth proposed on 2026-10-22: one popup, then JusPrin creates a fresh, empty, named calibration project; the\n22\tagent in that project asks which test; the test is generated into that same project. This requires that calling\n23\t`Plater::calib_temp`, `Plater::calib_flowrate` or `Plater::calib_pa` (`src/slic3r/GUI/Plater.cpp` from line 12677) in a\n24\tproject JusPrin just created behaves like a no-op for the project itself and like OrcaSlicer's own dialog for the test.\n25\t\n26\t## What was run\n27\t\n28\t- Branch `jusprin-newui` at `14fe07a078`, in worktree `.claude/worktrees/kind-mestorf-a37ba6`, Release build\n29\t  (`cmake --build build/arm64 --config Release --target workspace_adapter_integration_harness -- -j4`).\n30\t- A throwaway mode `--calibration` added to the real-adapter harness (`tests/workspace/real_adapter_harness.cpp`). It\n31\t  boots the real app with the JusPrin shell installed (the real `OrcaWorkspaceAdapter`, `ProjectPersistence`,\n32\t  `ProjectAutosave` and the shell's release hook). Printer `Bambu Lab X1 Carbon 0.4 nozzle`, filament\n33\t  `Bambu PLA Basic @BBL X1C`, process `0.20mm Standard @BBL X1C`. No model calls.\n34\t- Command: `JusPrinWorkspaceHarness.app/Contents/MacOS/JusPrinWorkspaceHarness --calibration`. Run three times; results\n35\t  repeated exactly. The probe code, the harness diff and the full log of the last run are kept in the shared notes\n36\t  folder, `calibration-generate-probe/` (`calibration_probe.inc`, `real_adapter_harness.diff`, `calib-run.log`). The\n37\t  probe was removed from the worktree afterwards.\n38\t\n39\t**JusPrin path** (runs J1 to J6). For each test: a person's project with a cube and an unsaved filament edit\n40\t(`nozzle_temperature=222`); then `IWorkspace::open_project({new_project=true})`, the exact call the `project_open`\n41\ttool makes with `new: true` (`OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp:2518`); then a name set with `Plater::set_project_filename(name)`;\n42\toptionally two chat messages (assistant \"Which test…?\", user \"temp\") written into the project's chat; then the\n43\tgenerator, called as OrcaSlicer's dialog calls it (`calib_dlg.cpp:308, 479, 1538`). Every modal dialog was intercepted,\n44\tcounted and refused.\n45\t\n46\t**OrcaSlicer path** (runs O_…). The shell's release hook removed (`set_before_project_release({})`), unsaved preset\n47\tedits dropped, `new_project(true, true)` for a fresh untitled project, then the same generator call. This is what a\n48\tperson gets from OrcaSlicer's own menu in a freshly started app.\n49\t\n50\tInputs: temperature tower 230 → 190; flow rate pass 1, non-linear (Archimedean chords); pressure advance pattern and\n51\ttower, 0 → 0.08 step 0.005. For the PA pattern the run also called `Plater::reslice()`, which writes the pattern's\n52\tper-layer G-code into the plate (`Plater.cpp:16066`).\n53\t\n54\tA run's \"content\" line is a fingerprint of the result: plates and their origins, every object (name, volumes, position,\n55\tsize, per-object settings), the print's `calib_params` on each plate, the PA pattern state, the plate's custom G-code\n56\t(item count, bytes, hash), and every edited preset option with its value.\n57\t\n58\t## Results by point\n59\t\n60\t### 1. The generator's own `new_project()` in a fresh JusPrin project\n61\t\n62\tNo dialog in any run, JusPrin or OrcaSlicer path: `CALIB J1_temp_untouched DIALOGS 0`, likewise J2 to J6.\n63\t\n64\tBut it is not a no-op. It replaces the project every time:\n65\t\n66\t```\n67\tCALIB J1_temp_untouched EMPTY     name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\" project_id=p-42d0a83e… session=4 active_messages=[] home_projects=2\n68\tCALIB J1_temp_untouched GENERATED name=\"Nozzle temperature test\" project_id=p-57457fde… session=5 active_messages=[note+file_report,] home_projects=2\n69\tHARNESS CHECK J1_temp_untouched_same_project_identity FAIL\n70\tHARNESS CHECK J1_temp_untouched_same_session FAIL\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\tThe JusPrin project id, the workspace session and the chat document all change. The same happened in every run.\n74\tReason, from the code: `new_project()` gives the model a new auxiliary directory, and `ProjectPersistence` treats that\n75\tas a project boundary and starts a fresh identity (`ProjectPersistence.cpp:181-185`, `start_fresh_identity`).\n76\t\n77\tWhether anything is saved to version history depends on the empty project's chat:\n78\t\n79\t- **Untouched empty project (J1):** nothing saved. `home_projects=2` before and after. The release hook calls\n80\t  `ProjectAutosave::save_now()`, which returns early for a project with no objects, no saved version and a chat\n81\t  revision of 1 or less (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:1024`).\n82\t- **Empty project with a chat (J2, J3, the realistic case):** the empty project is saved as a project of its own, and\n83\t  the chat stays in it:\n84\t\n85\t  ```\n86\t  CALIB J3_flow_chat EMPTY     name=\"Calibration flow (JusPrin name)\" project_id=p-67b48dcc… doc_revision=7 active_messages=[assistant,user,] home_projects=7 objects=0\n87\t  CALIB J3_flow_chat GENERATED name=\"Flowrate Test - Pass1\" project_id=p-bff2a653… doc_revision=2 active_messages=[] home_projects=8 objects=9\n88\t  ```\n89\t\n90\t  The eighth Home entry is the empty \"Calibration flow (JusPrin name)\" project holding the \"which test?\" chat. The test\n91\t  itself is in a new project whose chat is empty.\n92\t\n93\tSide observation: after the temperature tower, the new project's chat holds a note with a file report\n94\t(`active_messages=[note+file_report,]`, both paths), because the generator loads `temperature_tower.drc` through the\n95\tordinary model load, which JusPrin reports as a file the person opened (`AgentHost::on_file_loaded`). With an agent\n96\tconfigured that report may start a file-loaded turn (reading of `AgentHost.cpp` ~1660, not run). The flow and PA runs\n97\tposted no note; I did not trace why.\n98\t\n99\t### 2. The project name\n100\t\n101\tThe generator renames the project (`name=\"Nozzle temperature test\"`, `\"Flowrate Test - Pass1\"`,\n102\t`\"Pressure Advance Test\"`). JusPrin can set its own name back with the existing public\n103\t`Plater::set_project_filename(name)`; with a bare name (no folder) it changes only the name: `file=\"\"` stays empty and\n104\tnothing is added to recent files.\n105\t\n106\tWhether the name survives a save depends on order:\n107\t\n108\t- **Renamed before the first save (J1, J3, J4, J5, J6): survives.**\n109\t  `CALIB J1_temp_untouched NAMED name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\" … version_name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\"`\n110\t- **Saved once, then renamed (J2): does not.** The plater shows JusPrin's name, but the saved version and Home keep\n111\t  the generator's name:\n112\t  ```\n113\t  CALIB J2_temp_chat SAVED_BEFORE_RENAME name=\"Nozzle temperature test\" … version_name=\"Nozzle temperature test\"\n114\t  CALIB J2_temp_chat NAMED name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\" … version_name=\"Nozzle temperature test\"\n115\t  HARNESS CHECK J2_temp_chat_saved_version_name_is_jusprins FAIL\n116\t  ```\n117\t  `ProjectAutosave` fixes a project's name at its first saved version and reuses it afterwards\n118\t  (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:515-519`). So the rename must happen in the same call as the generator, before the autosave\n119\t  timer's first save.\n120\t\n121\t### 3. The test itself: identical to OrcaSlicer's own path\n122\t\n123\tAll five JusPrin runs matched the OrcaSlicer path exactly (`HARNESS CHECK J1…J5_matches_orca_path PASS`): the same\n124\tobjects on plate 0 (`plates=1 current=0`), the same per-object settings, the same edited presets, the same\n125\t`calib_params` on the plate's print:\n126\t\n127\t| Test | `calib_params` on the plate's print (both paths) | Preset edits (both paths) |\n128\t|---|---|---|\n129\t| Temperature | `mode=6 start=230 end=190` (Calib_Temp_Tower) | filament `nozzle_temperature=230; nozzle_temperature_initial_layer=230` |\n130\t| Flow, pass 1 | `mode=0` (none: the flow test sets nothing there; its ratios are per-object `print_flow_ratio`, 9 objects) | print `reduce_crossing_wall=1` (other edits equal the stock values) |\n131\t| PA pattern | `mode=2 start=0 end=0.08 step=0.005 numbers=1` | print `brim_type, initial_layer_line_width, initial_layer_speed, line_width, wall_loops`; filament `filament_retract_when_changing_layer=0, filament_wipe=0`; printer `retract_when_changing_layer=0, wipe=0` |\n132\t| PA tower | `mode=3 start=0 end=0.08 step=0.005` | filament `slow_down_layer_time=1` |\n133\t\n134\tThe preset edits land in the app-wide edited presets, as on OrcaSlicer's path. The person's own unsaved edit\n135\t(`nozzle_temperature=222`) was saved with their project and dropped by `open_project` before the generator ran\n136\t(EMPTY lines show `filament[…]{}`), so nothing of theirs leaks into the test.\n137\t\n138\tOne difference to know about: **an unsaved preset edit made in the empty project carries into the test** (J6: a\n139\t`filament_flow_ratio=0.9` edit made before the flow generator is still there afterwards,\n140\t`filament[Bambu PLA Basic @BBL X1C]{filament_flow_ratio=0.9;}`). On OrcaSlicer's path the person would be asked whether\n141\tto keep or discard edits; with JusPrin's release hook installed, `new_project()` skips that question\n142\t(`Plater.cpp:12077`) and keeps them. This is from reading for the OrcaSlicer side; the JusPrin side was run. It means\n143\tan agent that edits settings in the empty project before generating would change the test.\n144\t\n145\t### 4. The pressure-advance pattern\n146\t\n147\tSame in the already-open project: one plate, origin `(0.000,0.000)`, `pa_pattern=1`, and after `reslice()` the plate's\n148\tcustom G-code is `custom_gcode[plate0]=4 items, 91795 bytes, hash 7631567880486599293` on both paths. The generator's\n149\t`new_project()` always selects plate 0 first (`Plater.cpp:12120`), so the hard-coded first-plate origin\n150\t(`Plater.cpp:12814`) is right in both cases.\n151\t\n152\tThe first run showed a different byte count (91,795 vs 56,047). The two texts had the same 2,837 moves line for line\n153\tand differed only in comments. Cause: `GCodeWriter::full_gcode_comment` is a process-wide flag, on by default\n154\t(`GCodeWriter.cpp:21`), that the first finished G-code export sets from the print's `gcode_comments` setting\n155\t(`GCode.cpp:2038`). My JusPrin run sliced before any export had finished; the OrcaSlicer run came after one. With the\n156\tflag pinned before both runs the outputs are identical. This is OrcaSlicer's behaviour, not JusPrin's: the PA\n157\tpattern's comments depend on whether something was sliced earlier in the session. It does not change what prints.\n158\t\n159\t### 5. What would be needed\n160\t\n161\tOrcaSlicer's generators are fine as they are. No seam in OrcaSlicer-owned code is needed, and I recommend against one.\n162\tThe alternative, a flag that makes `calib_*` skip its own `new_project()`, would touch at least three functions in\n163\t`Plater.cpp` (the fork's busiest upstream file). It would also skip resets that `new_project()` performs (preset reload,\n164\t`calib_pa_pattern`, `plates_custom_gcodes`), so the test could differ from OrcaSlicer's. Under\n165\t`fork-stewardship.md` principles 2 and 3 that means owning upstream behaviour for no gain.\n166\t\n167\tProposed instead, all in fork-owned files, **not implemented and not run**:\n168\t\n169\t1. **Carry the identity across one replacement.** `ProjectPersistence` gets a one-shot \"the next project boundary\n170\t   continues this project\" call. While it is armed, `adopt_current_project` keeps the current document (project id,\n171\t   chats, setup record) instead of calling `start_fresh_identity`. `ProjectAutosave::adopt_project` then returns early\n172\t   because the id is unchanged (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:459`), so version history continues in the same store.\n173\t2. **Skip the release save for that replacement.** The shell's release hook (`ShellController.cpp:420`) does not save\n174\t   while the one-shot is armed: the same project continues, so no separate empty project reaches Home.\n175\t3. **The generate tool does it in one call:** arm the one-shot, call `calib_*` inside the adapter's existing tool-load\n176\t   scope (`ToolLoad`, as `open_project` uses) so the generator's model load is not reported as the person opening a\n177\t   file, then `set_project_filename(<JusPrin's name>)` before returning, and return the new `sessionId` as\n178\t   `project_open` does.\n179\t4. **One design point to settle:** `AgentHost` drops a tool activity whose session is no longer current\n180\t   (`AgentHost.cpp:715`: \"The last word of an action that replaced the project belongs to the project it closed\"). A\n181\t   generate call that keeps the project identity must still have its own result recorded in the chat that asked for\n182\t   it. The tool needs an explicit rule here; I did not test one.\n183\t\n184\tAlso decide whether the agent may edit presets in the empty project before generating (point 3: those edits carry into\n185\tthe test).\n186\t\n187\t## Not verified here\n188\t\n189\t- Anything with a live agent: a file-loaded turn after the temperature tower, the tool-result session filter in point\n190\t  5.4.\n191\t- Slicing the temperature tower to G-code. I read `calib_params` on the print; I did not slice.\n192\t- PA line mode, flow pass 2 and the linear (YOLO) flow test, multi-extruder printers, non-Bambu printers.\n193\t- Reopening a saved test (the handoff's fact 2 was not re-checked).\n194\t- That the person's preset edit is inside their saved version. The open path saved a version (Home count rose), but I\n195\t  did not open it to check.\n196\t\n197\t## Housekeeping\n198\t\n199\tThe worktree's branch `claude/kind-mestorf-a37ba6` was moved from the stale `095fb66576` to `14fe07a078`. Nothing was\n200\tcommitted. The harness removed its own data directories on exit. The Release build in `build/arm64` (about 1 GB) is\n201\tleft in place so the probe can be rerun; delete it when no longer wanted.\n202\t"}]},"uuid":"bd33f314-a5b3-4935-ade0-9518b68d8b9f","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:59:23.152Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-generate-verification.md","content":"# Can OrcaSlicer's calibration generators run into a project JusPrin already opened?\n\nVerified by running code on 2026-10-22. Untracked working document; do not commit.\n\n## Short answer\n\n**No, not as the flow is worded today; yes with one JusPrin-owned addition, and no OrcaSlicer change is needed.**\n\nOrcaSlicer's temperature, flow and pressure-advance generators never draw into the project that is open. Each one\nstarts its own new project. They do this silently: no dialog appears, and the test they build is identical to what\nOrcaSlicer's own dialogs build. But the project that holds the test is a different JusPrin project from the empty one\nJusPrin opened. It has a new project id, a new workspace session and an empty chat. If the empty project already had a\nchat (the agent asked \"which test?\"), that empty project is also saved and shows up on Home as a separate project.\n\nSo \"one popup → empty calibration project → the agent asks which test → generate into this project\" works only if\nJusPrin carries its own project identity (id, chat, name) across the generator's project replacement. That is a change\nin fork-owned code (`ProjectPersistence`, the release hook in `ShellController`, the generate tool), described at the end.\n\n## The hypothesis tested\n\nThe flow Kenneth proposed on 2026-10-22: one popup, then JusPrin creates a fresh, empty, named calibration project; the\nagent in that project asks which test; the test is generated into that same project. This requires that calling\n`Plater::calib_temp`, `Plater::calib_flowrate` or `Plater::calib_pa` (`src/slic3r/GUI/Plater.cpp` from line 12677) in a\nproject JusPrin just created behaves like a no-op for the project itself and like OrcaSlicer's own dialog for the test.\n\n## What was run\n\n- Branch `jusprin-newui` at `14fe07a078`, in worktree `.claude/worktrees/kind-mestorf-a37ba6`, Release build\n  (`cmake --build build/arm64 --config Release --target workspace_adapter_integration_harness -- -j4`).\n- A throwaway mode `--calibration` added to the real-adapter harness (`tests/workspace/real_adapter_harness.cpp`). It\n  boots the real app with the JusPrin shell installed (the real `OrcaWorkspaceAdapter`, `ProjectPersistence`,\n  `ProjectAutosave` and the shell's release hook). Printer `Bambu Lab X1 Carbon 0.4 nozzle`, filament\n  `Bambu PLA Basic @BBL X1C`, process `0.20mm Standard @BBL X1C`. No model calls.\n- Command: `JusPrinWorkspaceHarness.app/Contents/MacOS/JusPrinWorkspaceHarness --calibration`. Run three times; results\n  repeated exactly. The probe code, the harness diff and the full log of the last run are kept in the shared notes\n  folder, `calibration-generate-probe/` (`calibration_probe.inc`, `real_adapter_harness.diff`, `calib-run.log`). The\n  probe was removed from the worktree afterwards.\n\n**JusPrin path** (runs J1 to J6). For each test: a person's project with a cube and an unsaved filament edit\n(`nozzle_temperature=222`); then `IWorkspace::open_project({new_project=true})`, the exact call the `project_open`\ntool makes with `new: true` (`OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp:2518`); then a name set with `Plater::set_project_filename(name)`;\noptionally two chat messages (assistant \"Which test…?\", user \"temp\") written into the project's chat; then the\ngenerator, called as OrcaSlicer's dialog calls it (`calib_dlg.cpp:308, 479, 1538`). Every modal dialog was intercepted,\ncounted and refused.\n\n**OrcaSlicer path** (runs O_…). The shell's release hook removed (`set_before_project_release({})`), unsaved preset\nedits dropped, `new_project(true, true)` for a fresh untitled project, then the same generator call. This is what a\nperson gets from OrcaSlicer's own menu in a freshly started app.\n\nInputs: temperature tower 230 → 190; flow rate pass 1, non-linear (Archimedean chords); pressure advance pattern and\ntower, 0 → 0.08 step 0.005. For the PA pattern the run also called `Plater::reslice()`, which writes the pattern's\nper-layer G-code into the plate (`Plater.cpp:16066`).\n\nA run's \"content\" line is a fingerprint of the result: plates and their origins, every object (name, volumes, position,\nsize, per-object settings), the print's `calib_params` on each plate, the PA pattern state, the plate's custom G-code\n(item count, bytes, hash), and every edited preset option with its value.\n\n## Results by point\n\n### 1. The generator's own `new_project()` in a fresh JusPrin project\n\nNo dialog in any run, JusPrin or OrcaSlicer path: `CALIB J1_temp_untouched DIALOGS 0`, likewise J2 to J6.\n\nBut it is not a no-op. It replaces the project every time:\n\n```\nCALIB J1_temp_untouched EMPTY     name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\" project_id=p-42d0a83e… session=4 active_messages=[] home_projects=2\nCALIB J1_temp_untouched GENERATED name=\"Nozzle temperature test\" project_id=p-57457fde… session=5 active_messages=[note+file_report,] home_projects=2\nHARNESS CHECK J1_temp_untouched_same_project_identity FAIL\nHARNESS CHECK J1_temp_untouched_same_session FAIL\n```\n\nThe JusPrin project id, the workspace session and the chat document all change. The same happened in every run.\nReason, from the code: `new_project()` gives the model a new auxiliary directory, and `ProjectPersistence` treats that\nas a project boundary and starts a fresh identity (`ProjectPersistence.cpp:181-185`, `start_fresh_identity`).\n\nWhether anything is saved to version history depends on the empty project's chat:\n\n- **Untouched empty project (J1):** nothing saved. `home_projects=2` before and after. The release hook calls\n  `ProjectAutosave::save_now()`, which returns early for a project with no objects, no saved version and a chat\n  revision of 1 or less (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:1024`).\n- **Empty project with a chat (J2, J3, the realistic case):** the empty project is saved as a project of its own, and\n  the chat stays in it:\n\n  ```\n  CALIB J3_flow_chat EMPTY     name=\"Calibration flow (JusPrin name)\" project_id=p-67b48dcc… doc_revision=7 active_messages=[assistant,user,] home_projects=7 objects=0\n  CALIB J3_flow_chat GENERATED name=\"Flowrate Test - Pass1\" project_id=p-bff2a653… doc_revision=2 active_messages=[] home_projects=8 objects=9\n  ```\n\n  The eighth Home entry is the empty \"Calibration flow (JusPrin name)\" project holding the \"which test?\" chat. The test\n  itself is in a new project whose chat is empty.\n\nSide observation: after the temperature tower, the new project's chat holds a note with a file report\n(`active_messages=[note+file_report,]`, both paths), because the generator loads `temperature_tower.drc` through the\nordinary model load, which JusPrin reports as a file the person opened (`AgentHost::on_file_loaded`). With an agent\nconfigured that report may start a file-loaded turn (reading of `AgentHost.cpp` ~1660, not run). The flow and PA runs\nposted no note; I did not trace why.\n\n### 2. The project name\n\nThe generator renames the project (`name=\"Nozzle temperature test\"`, `\"Flowrate Test - Pass1\"`,\n`\"Pressure Advance Test\"`). JusPrin can set its own name back with the existing public\n`Plater::set_project_filename(name)`; with a bare name (no folder) it changes only the name: `file=\"\"` stays empty and\nnothing is added to recent files.\n\nWhether the name survives a save depends on order:\n\n- **Renamed before the first save (J1, J3, J4, J5, J6): survives.**\n  `CALIB J1_temp_untouched NAMED name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\" … version_name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\"`\n- **Saved once, then renamed (J2): does not.** The plater shows JusPrin's name, but the saved version and Home keep\n  the generator's name:\n  ```\n  CALIB J2_temp_chat SAVED_BEFORE_RENAME name=\"Nozzle temperature test\" … version_name=\"Nozzle temperature test\"\n  CALIB J2_temp_chat NAMED name=\"Calibration temp (JusPrin name)\" … version_name=\"Nozzle temperature test\"\n  HARNESS CHECK J2_temp_chat_saved_version_name_is_jusprins FAIL\n  ```\n  `ProjectAutosave` fixes a project's name at its first saved version and reuses it afterwards\n  (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:515-519`). So the rename must happen in the same call as the generator, before the autosave\n  timer's first save.\n\n### 3. The test itself: identical to OrcaSlicer's own path\n\nAll five JusPrin runs matched the OrcaSlicer path exactly (`HARNESS CHECK J1…J5_matches_orca_path PASS`): the same\nobjects on plate 0 (`plates=1 current=0`), the same per-object settings, the same edited presets, the same\n`calib_params` on the plate's print:\n\n| Test | `calib_params` on the plate's print (both paths) | Preset edits (both paths) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Temperature | `mode=6 start=230 end=190` (Calib_Temp_Tower) | filament `nozzle_temperature=230; nozzle_temperature_initial_layer=230` |\n| Flow, pass 1 | `mode=0` (none: the flow test sets nothing there; its ratios are per-object `print_flow_ratio`, 9 objects) | print `reduce_crossing_wall=1` (other edits equal the stock values) |\n| PA pattern | `mode=2 start=0 end=0.08 step=0.005 numbers=1` | print `brim_type, initial_layer_line_width, initial_layer_speed, line_width, wall_loops`; filament `filament_retract_when_changing_layer=0, filament_wipe=0`; printer `retract_when_changing_layer=0, wipe=0` |\n| PA tower | `mode=3 start=0 end=0.08 step=0.005` | filament `slow_down_layer_time=1` |\n\nThe preset edits land in the app-wide edited presets, as on OrcaSlicer's path. The person's own unsaved edit\n(`nozzle_temperature=222`) was saved with their project and dropped by `open_project` before the generator ran\n(EMPTY lines show `filament[…]{}`), so nothing of theirs leaks into the test.\n\nOne difference to know about: **an unsaved preset edit made in the empty project carries into the test** (J6: a\n`filament_flow_ratio=0.9` edit made before the flow generator is still there afterwards,\n`filament[Bambu PLA Basic @BBL X1C]{filament_flow_ratio=0.9;}`). On OrcaSlicer's path the person would be asked whether\nto keep or discard edits; with JusPrin's release hook installed, `new_project()` skips that question\n(`Plater.cpp:12077`) and keeps them. This is from reading for the OrcaSlicer side; the JusPrin side was run. It means\nan agent that edits settings in the empty project before generating would change the test.\n\n### 4. The pressure-advance pattern\n\nSame in the already-open project: one plate, origin `(0.000,0.000)`, `pa_pattern=1`, and after `reslice()` the plate's\ncustom G-code is `custom_gcode[plate0]=4 items, 91795 bytes, hash 7631567880486599293` on both paths. The generator's\n`new_project()` always selects plate 0 first (`Plater.cpp:12120`), so the hard-coded first-plate origin\n(`Plater.cpp:12814`) is right in both cases.\n\nThe first run showed a different byte count (91,795 vs 56,047). The two texts had the same 2,837 moves line for line\nand differed only in comments. Cause: `GCodeWriter::full_gcode_comment` is a process-wide flag, on by default\n(`GCodeWriter.cpp:21`), that the first finished G-code export sets from the print's `gcode_comments` setting\n(`GCode.cpp:2038`). My JusPrin run sliced before any export had finished; the OrcaSlicer run came after one. With the\nflag pinned before both runs the outputs are identical. This is OrcaSlicer's behaviour, not JusPrin's: the PA\npattern's comments depend on whether something was sliced earlier in the session. It does not change what prints.\n\n### 5. What would be needed\n\nOrcaSlicer's generators are fine as they are. No seam in OrcaSlicer-owned code is needed, and I recommend against one.\nThe alternative, a flag that makes `calib_*` skip its own `new_project()`, would touch at least three functions in\n`Plater.cpp` (the fork's busiest upstream file). It would also skip resets that `new_project()` performs (preset reload,\n`calib_pa_pattern`, `plates_custom_gcodes`), so the test could differ from OrcaSlicer's. Under\n`fork-stewardship.md` principles 2 and 3 that means owning upstream behaviour for no gain.\n\nProposed instead, all in fork-owned files, **not implemented and not run**:\n\n1. **Carry the identity across one replacement.** `ProjectPersistence` gets a one-shot \"the next project boundary\n   continues this project\" call. While it is armed, `adopt_current_project` keeps the current document (project id,\n   chats, setup record) instead of calling `start_fresh_identity`. `ProjectAutosave::adopt_project` then returns early\n   because the id is unchanged (`ProjectAutosave.cpp:459`), so version history continues in the same store.\n2. **Skip the release save for that replacement.** The shell's release hook (`ShellController.cpp:420`) does not save\n   while the one-shot is armed: the same project continues, so no separate empty project reaches Home.\n3. **The generate tool does it in one call:** arm the one-shot, call `calib_*` inside the adapter's existing tool-load\n   scope (`ToolLoad`, as `open_project` uses) so the generator's model load is not reported as the person opening a\n   file, then `set_project_filename(<JusPrin's name>)` before returning, and return the new `sessionId` as\n   `project_open` does.\n4. **One design point to settle:** `AgentHost` drops a tool activity whose session is no longer current\n   (`AgentHost.cpp:715`: \"The last word of an action that replaced the project belongs to the project it closed\"). A\n   generate call that keeps the project identity must still have its own result recorded in the chat that asked for\n   it. The tool needs an explicit rule here; I did not test one.\n\nAlso decide whether the agent may edit presets in the empty project before generating (point 3: those edits carry into\nthe test).\n\n## Not verified here\n\n- Anything with a live agent: a file-loaded turn after the temperature tower, the tool-result session filter in point\n  5.4.\n- Slicing the temperature tower to G-code. I read `calib_params` on the print; I did not slice.\n- PA line mode, flow pass 2 and the linear (YOLO) flow test, multi-extruder printers, non-Bambu printers.\n- Reopening a saved test (the handoff's fact 2 was not re-checked).\n- That the person's preset edit is inside their saved version. The open path saved a version (Home count rose), but I\n  did not open it to check.\n\n## Housekeeping\n\nThe worktree's branch `claude/kind-mestorf-a37ba6` was moved from the stale `095fb66576` to `14fe07a078`. Nothing was\ncommitted. The harness removed its own data directories on exit. The Release build in `build/arm64` (about 1 GB) is\nleft in place so the probe can be rerun; delete it when no longer wanted.\n","numLines":202,"startLine":1,"totalLines":202}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5886aee2-f4e3-450a-ade7-c92337fba237","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
{"parentUuid":"bd33f314-a5b3-4935-ade0-9518b68d8b9f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJVvg9MzwcC3q7oQRT1t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013JKvfqEGD8ZxrE2yWaN5L3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-session-brief.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1801,"cache_read_input_tokens":560869,"output_tokens":252,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":109},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1801,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":252,"cache_read_input_tokens":560869,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1801,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1801},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013JKvfqEGD8ZxrE2yWaN5L3":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-session-brief.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgJVvfpGA5fdrHZth3GUz","requestRef":"849d0eea-ce74-4933-ba17-ca2ced7ddc83","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"94f555fa-dbbd-4e39-858b-0ebee28ffcf4","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:59:23.491Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
{"parentUuid":"94f555fa-dbbd-4e39-858b-0ebee28ffcf4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"46b3d61b-0340-48d5-8c60-f5a20be79cd7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013JKvfqEGD8ZxrE2yWaN5L3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Brief: implement calibration from the handoff (Windows VM edition)\n2\t\n3\tPaste this into a Claude Code session on the VM `jusprin-win-test` as `guitest`, in a checkout of\n4\tTheSpaghettiDetective/JusPrin on branch `jusprin-newui`.\n5\t\n6\t## Background\n7\t\n8\tJusPrin is a long-lived fork of OrcaSlicer (C++17, wxWidgets) with a React Agent pane; the product is an\n9\tAI-piloted slicer where the agent does the technical work through typed tools and the person keeps physical facts\n10\tand consequential actions. On 2026-10-21 Kenneth decided how calibration works: a calibration is a project of its\n11\town (OrcaSlicer's generators call `new_project()` and edit project-wide presets, so a plate in the current project is\n12\timpossible); the first tests are temperature, flow and pressure advance; every result is a new preset whose name the\n13\tperson is told to use from now on, with no switching by the app; no remembered return to the previous project; no\n14\tper-test UI, adding a test is a declaration plus skill text; one popup naming filament and printer, then an empty\n15\tcalibration project whose agent picks the test and generates into it (the generators replace the project, verified by\n16\trunning on 2026-10-22, so JusPrin carries the project identity across that replacement). The UX is drawn on the JusPrin v2 canvas\n17\t(https://claude.ai/artifact/VCEppdwfT7v32W4beUxL1v, page \"Calibration · Printer & Filament\"). The handoff at\n18\t`C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md` holds the verified facts with file and line pointers, the exact\n19\tbuild, the tests required and the open questions; it is the single source for this task. Temporary docs stay\n20\tuntracked and are never committed or gitignored.\n21\t\n22\t## Task\n23\t\n24\tImplement printer and filament calibration in JusPrin exactly as `C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md`\n25\tspecifies. Read it in full first, then `agent-docs/jusprin/README.md` and the documents it lists, especially\n26\t`fork-stewardship.md` before touching any file outside `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/`. Windows build and run recipe:\n27\t`BUILDING.md` and `README.md` in `C:\\guitest` (run long builds as a scheduled task, never over an SSH session that can\n28\tdrop; force `GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe` to run the app headless).\n29\t\n30\tBuild, in this order, and stop to report after each step with tests passing:\n31\t\n32\t1. The `calibration_generate` tool (handoff section 5.1): registry entry in\n33\t   `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ToolRegistry.cpp`, handler in `ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp`, adapter work in\n34\t   `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Workspace/OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp` reusing the project-release path `project_open` uses.\n35\t   Temperature, flow and pressure advance only. Catch2 tests in `tests/workspace` as section 6 lists them.\n36\t2. The `calibration` skill at `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/AgentUI/src/skills/calibration/SKILL.md` (section 5.2), with a\n37\t   one-line pointer added to `skills/print-issues/SKILL.md`, and Vitest coverage that it parses and is listed by\n38\t   description. Rebuild the web bundle as the AgentUI README says.\n39\t3. The three \"Calibrate…\" entry rows and the one popup (section 5.3) in `Shell/PrinterMenu.cpp`, `Shell/FilamentMenu.cpp` and\n40\t   `HomeUI/src/components/PrinterMenu.tsx` with its host action: the Agent page's own Confirmation dialog naming the filament\n41\t   and printer, then the app creates the empty, named, marked calibration project through the `project_open` path, opens its\n42\t   chat with the handover system line and fires the `calibration_started` trigger. The generate tool continues that same\n43\t   project with the carry-over of section 5.1 (ProjectPersistence one-shot, release-hook skip, rename before the first\n44\t   autosave, the AgentHost result exemption). Native rows styled only through `ShellTheme` and the tokens. The no-agent case\n45\t   is undecided: build nothing for it.\n46\t4. Correct `agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`'s calibration table as section 5 says.\n47\t\n48\tDo not build `calibration_list`, per-test cards, preset switching, a return-to-project hook, or Bambu's automatic\n49\tpath. Measure option (b) of section 5.4 before choosing a seam and say what you found. Leave the three open questions\n50\tof section 7 to Kenneth: ask them in your report, do not decide them.\n51\t\n52\tDone looks like: the tests in section 6 exist and pass (ctest on the workspace suite, Vitest in `AgentUI`), a\n53\tshell-harness run with `--manual-mcp` drives `calibration_generate` and the settings tools end to end on the fake\n54\tBambu printer (`src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md`), and a cold-reader report in the checkout root (untracked)\n55\tsays what was built, what was verified and how, and what was left out. No live-model runs without Kenneth approving a\n56\tspecific batch. Commit only code, tests and standing docs; never the report or the handoff. MSVC trap to remember:\n57\tnever capture `this` in a nested lambda's init-capture; name the object first.\n58\t"}]},"uuid":"4b58b81b-aaeb-4d45-ae38-af3bc9d5cc56","timestamp":"2026-10-23T03:59:23.565Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-session-brief.md","content":"# Brief: implement calibration from the handoff (Windows VM edition)\n\nPaste this into a Claude Code session on the VM `jusprin-win-test` as `guitest`, in a checkout of\nTheSpaghettiDetective/JusPrin on branch `jusprin-newui`.\n\n## Background\n\nJusPrin is a long-lived fork of OrcaSlicer (C++17, wxWidgets) with a React Agent pane; the product is an\nAI-piloted slicer where the agent does the technical work through typed tools and the person keeps physical facts\nand consequential actions. On 2026-10-21 Kenneth decided how calibration works: a calibration is a project of its\nown (OrcaSlicer's generators call `new_project()` and edit project-wide presets, so a plate in the current project is\nimpossible); the first tests are temperature, flow and pressure advance; every result is a new preset whose name the\nperson is told to use from now on, with no switching by the app; no remembered return to the previous project; no\nper-test UI, adding a test is a declaration plus skill text; one popup naming filament and printer, then an empty\ncalibration project whose agent picks the test and generates into it (the generators replace the project, verified by\nrunning on 2026-10-22, so JusPrin carries the project identity across that replacement). The UX is drawn on the JusPrin v2 canvas\n(https://claude.ai/artifact/VCEppdwfT7v32W4beUxL1v, page \"Calibration · Printer & Filament\"). The handoff at\n`C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md` holds the verified facts with file and line pointers, the exact\nbuild, the tests required and the open questions; it is the single source for this task. Temporary docs stay\nuntracked and are never committed or gitignored.\n\n## Task\n\nImplement printer and filament calibration in JusPrin exactly as `C:\\Users\\guitest\\Downloads\\calibration-handoff.md`\nspecifies. Read it in full first, then `agent-docs/jusprin/README.md` and the documents it lists, especially\n`fork-stewardship.md` before touching any file outside `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/`. Windows build and run recipe:\n`BUILDING.md` and `README.md` in `C:\\guitest` (run long builds as a scheduled task, never over an SSH session that can\ndrop; force `GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe` to run the app headless).\n\nBuild, in this order, and stop to report after each step with tests passing:\n\n1. The `calibration_generate` tool (handoff section 5.1): registry entry in\n   `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Agent/ToolRegistry.cpp`, handler in `ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp`, adapter work in\n   `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Workspace/OrcaWorkspaceAdapter.cpp` reusing the project-release path `project_open` uses.\n   Temperature, flow and pressure advance only. Catch2 tests in `tests/workspace` as section 6 lists them.\n2. The `calibration` skill at `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/AgentUI/src/skills/calibration/SKILL.md` (section 5.2), with a\n   one-line pointer added to `skills/print-issues/SKILL.md`, and Vitest coverage that it parses and is listed by\n   description. Rebuild the web bundle as the AgentUI README says.\n3. The three \"Calibrate…\" entry rows and the one popup (section 5.3) in `Shell/PrinterMenu.cpp`, `Shell/FilamentMenu.cpp` and\n   `HomeUI/src/components/PrinterMenu.tsx` with its host action: the Agent page's own Confirmation dialog naming the filament\n   and printer, then the app creates the empty, named, marked calibration project through the `project_open` path, opens its\n   chat with the handover system line and fires the `calibration_started` trigger. The generate tool continues that same\n   project with the carry-over of section 5.1 (ProjectPersistence one-shot, release-hook skip, rename before the first\n   autosave, the AgentHost result exemption). Native rows styled only through `ShellTheme` and the tokens. The no-agent case\n   is undecided: build nothing for it.\n4. Correct `agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`'s calibration table as section 5 says.\n\nDo not build `calibration_list`, per-test cards, preset switching, a return-to-project hook, or Bambu's automatic\npath. Measure option (b) of section 5.4 before choosing a seam and say what you found. Leave the three open questions\nof section 7 to Kenneth: ask them in your report, do not decide them.\n\nDone looks like: the tests in section 6 exist and pass (ctest on the workspace suite, Vitest in `AgentUI`), a\nshell-harness run with `--manual-mcp` drives `calibration_generate` and the settings tools end to end on the fake\nBambu printer (`src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md`), and a cold-reader report in the checkout root (untracked)\nsays what was built, what was verified and how, and what was left out. No live-model runs without Kenneth approving a\nspecific batch. Commit only code, tests and standing docs; never the report or the handoff. MSVC trap to remember:\nnever capture `this` in a nested lambda's init-capture; name the object first.\n","numLines":58,"startLine":1,"totalLines":58}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"94f555fa-dbbd-4e39-858b-0ebee28ffcf4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
