{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:30:04.998Z","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","content":"\n\n<pasted_content id=\"53a8\">\nIts “survives” and “needs rework” lists are right. One of them is better than the handoff: it will remove the forwarder it had added to OrcaSlicer’s Plater and use the existing public set_project_filename, which takes the branch’s only change to an OrcaSlicer-owned file back to zero.\n\nDisagreements that are correct and mine to fix in the handoff:\n\nThe turn would be cut off at the boundary. Verified in the code just now: adopting a document at a project boundary calls the host’s replaced-document handler, which cancels the agent, clears every tool call and continuation, and drops live work (AgentHost.cpp:793). The one-shot must suppress that notification as well as the session check I named. This is the most valuable catch in its reply.\nSaving a result. The agent is right that my section 5.2 contradicts my own board 5. The “Save preset” button sits on a card drawn from a tool result, so pressing it is the app’s action, not the agent’s; the agent previews, the app applies with the copy name on the press. The handoff must say so.\nWhich chat gets the result on the symptom path. Sections 5.1 and 6 disagree, as it says. Its resolution is the right one: the person’s chat keeps the last word as the open tool does today, the calibration chat opens with the handover line, and the tool gains an optional symptom argument so the handover can name it.\nThree names for one test. Right. One name everywhere, the project’s: “Calibration · Gray PETG · Prusa MK4”, including the chat title and board 8’s card. Board 4’s chat title and board 8’s card are my illustrations and will change.\nHandover wording. Right; the board’s shorter line wins.\nThe moved line numbers. Right, and harmless.\n\nDisagreements that rest on decisions you made in that session, which my handoff never saw. Q1 (ask the person whether to write into their own preset or save a copy), Q2 (the tool is offered over MCP too), Q3 (option a, board 7 as drawn), and the board 5 saving rule above. If those are your decisions, they go into the handoff as decisions and the open-questions list shrinks to the no-agent case. Say so and I’ll write them in.\n\nOne point where I’d push back. For the Home card path it proposes guessing the filament: the printer’s default, or its first loaded spool. The product definition keeps physical facts with the person, and which spool is in the printer is one. Better: the popup from a Home card names the printer only, the project is “Calibration · Prusa MK4”, and the handover tells the agent the filament is unknown, so its first question offers the loaded spools as reply chips, with the test question after. The agent also noticed something the handoff omits: from Home there is no open project, so the app must select the card’s printer in the new project itself; SetupCommands::select_named_printer exists for that.\n\nOn your word I’ll update the handoff for all of the above, redraw boards 4 and 8 for the single name, and copy both to the VM.\n</pasted_content id=\"53a8\">\n"}
