{"parentUuid":"cdecab6d-b08a-46a9-aba3-45800248b443","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a2d19e6f-cea9-4c9e-bae6-6360cca68abc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016sWjpTQ28RtYshRDtTAXq7","type":"tool_result","content":"75:A fixed, mechanical step of a workflow can be a tool. Examples are generating OrcaSlicer's calibration geometry and starting OrcaSlicer's slice action. Advice should stay a skill until there is a separately specified operation behind it that always behaves the same way.\n93:A skill is a file on the Agent page, `AgentUI/src/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`: frontmatter with its `name` and a `trigger`, a `description` or both, then the text the model gets. The page sends every skill once per connection with the `skills` bridge message. A skill with a trigger is the app's: when the event it names happens, the host adds it to the one request where the model first reads about the event, as the request's last item, and to no later request. A skill with a description is the model's: `skill_read` lists the described skills in its own description and returns one by name, and is offered only while at least one exists. One skill has a description: `calibration`, read when a print problem may come from the filament or its settings, when the person asks to calibrate, or in a calibration test's project; it carries the conversation of a calibration (when a test is worth it, the two facts said before the open project closes, per-test ranges and how to read the print, the naming rule for the saved preset), while `calibration_generate`'s description keeps only that tool's rules. Four skills have triggers, one file each:\n476:| `calibration` | 1 | Calibration tests, each a project of its own. |\n549:| `preflight_check` | R | For a plate and destination: job name, estimates, proposed filament-to-slot mapping with problems, plate and nozzle match, options with defaults and printer support (bed leveling, flow calibration, timelapse, first-layer inspection, plate detection, tangle detection, monitoring level), blocking problems, disabled-check warnings, applicable safety notes, confirmed facts. Returns a `preflightId` bound to project revision, slice identity, printer `observedAt`, and options, with an expiry. | mapping and readiness logic lifted from `SelectMachineDialog` behind a product-neutral seam; `MachineObject` | 16 mapping rows, problems, warnings |\n561:### `calibration`\n565:| `calibration_generate` | M | Implemented, in-app and over MCP. Replace the open project with one of OrcaSlicer's calibration tests (temperature, flow, pressure advance) as a project of its own: Orca's generators call `new_project()` and edit the project-wide presets, so a test cannot be a plate of another project. The open project is first released through `project_open`'s path (saved to its version history, unsaved preset edits dropped); a range Orca's dialog refuses is refused before anything closes. Returns the plate, its objects and the range applied, read back from the plate's print, and a summary sentence; for the in-app Agent the test's project opens its chat with it. The per-layer steps are runtime-only in Orca, so each plate reports them as `calibration` while its print holds them. Analysis is reasoning over a photo; a result is saved with `settings_apply_patch` on the filament scope with `persistAs`, a named copy no other project is switched to. | `Plater::calib_temp`, `calib_flowrate`, `calib_pa` | 64 objects |\n567:`calibration_list` was dropped (Kenneth, 2026-10-21): which test fits, its ranges and how to read it are static knowledge in the `calibration` skill, and the workspace already reports the printer, filament and nozzle. Bambu's automatic flow-dynamics calibration, whose results stay on the printer, is not a tool.\n621:3. The user reports stringing on a Bambu printer. Judge that a temperature tower is worth it, say that the open project will be saved and closed before generating it, read the user's photo, propose a filament temperature, then preview it and apply it with `persistAs` under the calibrated-copy name. Needs `calibration`, the `calibration` skill and the filament scope.\n808:copy (a calibration result without the test's own edits) can give those values","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0bb431eb-7676-4d51-b52d-5668a2b0d0fa","timestamp":"2026-10-23T05:17:04.260Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"75:A fixed, mechanical step of a workflow can be a tool. Examples are generating OrcaSlicer's calibration geometry and starting OrcaSlicer's slice action. Advice should stay a skill until there is a separately specified operation behind it that always behaves the same way.\n93:A skill is a file on the Agent page, `AgentUI/src/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`: frontmatter with its `name` and a `trigger`, a `description` or both, then the text the model gets. The page sends every skill once per connection with the `skills` bridge message. A skill with a trigger is the app's: when the event it names happens, the host adds it to the one request where the model first reads about the event, as the request's last item, and to no later request. A skill with a description is the model's: `skill_read` lists the described skills in its own description and returns one by name, and is offered only while at least one exists. One skill has a description: `calibration`, read when a print problem may come from the filament or its settings, when the person asks to calibrate, or in a calibration test's project; it carries the conversation of a calibration (when a test is worth it, the two facts said before the open project closes, per-test ranges and how to read the print, the naming rule for the saved preset), while `calibration_generate`'s description keeps only that tool's rules. Four skills have triggers, one file each:\n476:| `calibration` | 1 | Calibration tests, each a project of its own. |\n549:| `preflight_check` | R | For a plate and destination: job name, estimates, proposed filament-to-slot mapping with problems, plate and nozzle match, options with defaults and printer support (bed leveling, flow calibration, timelapse, first-layer inspection, plate detection, tangle detection, monitoring level), blocking problems, disabled-check warnings, applicable safety notes, confirmed facts. Returns a `preflightId` bound to project revision, slice identity, printer `observedAt`, and options, with an expiry. | mapping and readiness logic lifted from `SelectMachineDialog` behind a product-neutral seam; `MachineObject` | 16 mapping rows, problems, warnings |\n561:### `calibration`\n565:| `calibration_generate` | M | Implemented, in-app and over MCP. Replace the open project with one of OrcaSlicer's calibration tests (temperature, flow, pressure advance) as a project of its own: Orca's generators call `new_project()` and edit the project-wide presets, so a test cannot be a plate of another project. The open project is first released through `project_open`'s path (saved to its version history, unsaved preset edits dropped); a range Orca's dialog refuses is refused before anything closes. Returns the plate, its objects and the range applied, read back from the plate's print, and a summary sentence; for the in-app Agent the test's project opens its chat with it. The per-layer steps are runtime-only in Orca, so each plate reports them as `calibration` while its print holds them. Analysis is reasoning over a photo; a result is saved with `settings_apply_patch` on the filament scope with `persistAs`, a named copy no other project is switched to. | `Plater::calib_temp`, `calib_flowrate`, `calib_pa` | 64 objects |\n567:`calibration_list` was dropped (Kenneth, 2026-10-21): which test fits, its ranges and how to read it are static knowledge in the `calibration` skill, and the workspace already reports the printer, filament and nozzle. Bambu's automatic flow-dynamics calibration, whose results stay on the printer, is not a tool.\n621:3. The user reports stringing on a Bambu printer. Judge that a temperature tower is worth it, say that the open project will be saved and closed before generating it, read the user's photo, propose a filament temperature, then preview it and apply it with `persistAs` under the calibrated-copy name. Needs `calibration`, the `calibration` skill and the filament scope.\n808:copy (a calibration result without the test's own edits) can give those values","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cdecab6d-b08a-46a9-aba3-45800248b443","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\src\\JusPrin\\.claude\\worktrees\\calibration","sessionId":"e7286644-9c40-4163-89b2-1b9685541258","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"steady-sparking-swan"}
