t_filename`); the call takes the session it made, so its result lands in the chat that asked. From any other project it first releases that project through `project_open`'s path (saved to its version history, unsaved preset edits dropped) and opens the calibration project \"Calibration · <filament> · <printer>\" with its handover line, an optional `symptom` naming what the person saw; that chat's first turn starts once the test is made, and the call stays with the project it closed. A range Orca's dialog refuses is refused before anything closes. Returns the project's name, the plate, its objects and the range applied, read back from the plate's print, a summary sentence and the new session. The per-layer steps are runtime-only in Orca, so each plate reports them as `calibration` while its print holds them. Analysis is reasoning over a photo; a result is proposed with `settings_preview_patch` and `persistAs` and saved by the person's Save preset on the card the preview draws, a named copy no other project is switched to. | `Plater::calib_temp`, `calib_flowrate`, `calib_pa` | 64 objects |\"\"\"),\n(\"\"\"3. The user reports stringing on a Bambu printer. Judge that a temperature tower is worth it, say that the open project will be saved and closed before generating it, read the user's photo, propose a filament temperature, then preview it and apply it with `persistAs` under the calibrated-copy name. Needs `calibration`, the `calibration` skill and the filament scope.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"3. The user reports stringing on a Bambu printer. Judge that a temperature tower is worth it, say that the open project will be saved and closed before generating it, read the user's photo in the calibration project, and propose a filament temperature as a preview with `persistAs` under the calibrated-copy name, which the user saves from its card. Needs `calibration`, the `calibration` skill and the filament scope.\"\"\"),\n])\nprint(\"ok\")\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use"